 | $0$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(x-1)=2\\cdot (2-1)=2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot 1=2 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+2=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $3$ | $4$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 3.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the third row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(x-1)=4\\cdot (3-1)=8 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=8\\cdot 1=8 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=3+1=4 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=4+8=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table. \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $3$ | $4$ | $8$ | $8$ |\n| $4$ | $12$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(4)≈12.$" "First, note that we are given $y(1)$ and asked to find $y(4).$ The distance from $x=1$ and $x=4$ is $3,$ and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-1}{3}=\\frac{3}{3}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=2.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(x-1)=2\\cdot (1-1)=0 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 1=0 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+0=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(x-1)=2\\cdot (2-1)=2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot 1=2 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+2=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $3$ | $4$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 3.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the third row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(x-1)=4\\cdot (3-1)=8 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=8\\cdot 1=8 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=3+1=4 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=4+8=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table. \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $3$ | $4$ | $8$ | $8$ |\n| $4$ | $12$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(4)≈12.$"] [:db/cas 17592186256591 :question/problem "John collects a basket of apples, only $80%$ of them are ripe. The probability that a ripe apple has a worm inside the apple is $0.6.$  The probability that an unripe apple has a worm inside the apple is $0.1.$ What is the probability that a randomly selected apple in John's collection has a worm inside?" "John collects a basket of apples, only $80\\%$ of them are ripe. The probability that a ripe apple has a worm inside the apple is $0.6.$  The probability that an unripe apple has a worm inside the apple is $0.1.$ What is the probability that a randomly selected apple in John's collection has a worm inside?"] [:db/cas 17592186256591 :question/worked-solution "Let $W$ be the event that the chosen apple has a worm inside, and let $R$ be the event that the chosen apple is ripe.  \n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected apple in John's collection has a worm inside, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(W)=P(W|R)P(R)+P(W|{R}^{′})P({R}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $80%$ of the apples are ripe and $20$ are unripe, we have\n\n$$\nP(R)=0.8,\\,P({R}^{′})=0.2.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a ripe apple has a worm inside the apple is $0.6,$ and the probability that an unripe apple has a worm inside the apple is $0.1,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(W|R)=0.6,\\,P(W|{R}^{′})=0.1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected apple in John's collection has a worm inside is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W) & =P(W|R)P(R)+P(W|{R}^{′})P({R}^{′}) \\\\  & =0.6\\cdot 0.8+0.1\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & =0.48+0.02 \\\\  & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $W$ be the event that the chosen apple has a worm inside, and let $R$ be the event that the chosen apple is ripe.  \n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected apple in John's collection has a worm inside, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(W)=P(W|R)P(R)+P(W|{R}^{′})P({R}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $80\\%$ of the apples are ripe and $20$ are unripe, we have\n\n$$\nP(R)=0.8,\\,P({R}^{′})=0.2.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a ripe apple has a worm inside the apple is $0.6,$ and the probability that an unripe apple has a worm inside the apple is $0.1,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(W|R)=0.6,\\,P(W|{R}^{′})=0.1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected apple in John's collection has a worm inside is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W) & =P(W|R)P(R)+P(W|{R}^{′})P({R}^{′}) \\\\  & =0.6\\cdot 0.8+0.1\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & =0.48+0.02 \\\\  & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186256595 :question/problem "Two machines ${M}_{1}$ and ${M}_{2}$ in a certain factory produce $75%$ and $25%,$ respectively, of the total daily output of electronic tubes. It is known that $4%$ of the tubes produced by machine ${M}_{1}$ and that $5%$ of those produced by ${M}_{2}$ are defective. What is the probability that a randomly picked tube from a day's production is defective?" "Two machines ${M}_{1}$ and ${M}_{2}$ in a certain factory produce $75\\%$ and $25\\%,$ respectively, of the total daily output of electronic tubes. It is known that $4\\%$ of the tubes produced by machine ${M}_{1}$ and that $5\\%$ of those produced by ${M}_{2}$ are defective. What is the probability that a randomly picked tube from a day's production is defective?"] [:db/cas 17592186256595 :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the event that the picked electronic tube is defective, and let $A$ be the event that the electronic tube is produced by machine ${M}_{1}.$ Then, ${A}^{′}$ is the event that the electronic tube is produced by machine ${M}_{2}.$\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly picked tube from a day's production is defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(D)=P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since machine ${M}_{1}$ produces $75%$ of the total daily output of electronic tubes and ${M}_{2}$ the remaining $25%,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.75,\\,P({A}^{′})=0.25.\n$$\n\n- Since $4%$ of the tubes produced by machine ${M}_{1}$ are defective, and $5%$ of the tubes produced by machine ${M}_{2}$ are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.04,\\,P(D|{A}^{′})=0.05.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly picked tube from a day's production is defective is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′}) \\\\  & =(0.04)(0.75)+(0.05)(0.25) \\\\  & =0.0425.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $D$ be the event that the picked electronic tube is defective, and let $A$ be the event that the electronic tube is produced by machine ${M}_{1}.$ Then, ${A}^{′}$ is the event that the electronic tube is produced by machine ${M}_{2}.$\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly picked tube from a day's production is defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(D)=P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since machine ${M}_{1}$ produces $75\\%$ of the total daily output of electronic tubes and ${M}_{2}$ the remaining $25\\%,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.75,\\,P({A}^{′})=0.25.\n$$\n\n- Since $4\\%$ of the tubes produced by machine ${M}_{1}$ are defective, and $5\\%$ of the tubes produced by machine ${M}_{2}$ are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.04,\\,P(D|{A}^{′})=0.05.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly picked tube from a day's production is defective is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′}) \\\\  & =(0.04)(0.75)+(0.05)(0.25) \\\\  & =0.0425.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186256599 :question/problem "In a class, $20$ students are boys, and $24$ are girls. $5%$ of the boys are engaged in a sports section, and $25%$ of the girls are engaged in a sports section. What is the probability that a randomly selected student is engaged in a sports section?" "In a class, $20$ students are boys, and $24$ are girls. $5\\%$ of the boys are engaged in a sports section, and $25\\%$ of the girls are engaged in a sports section. What is the probability that a randomly selected student is engaged in a sports section?"] [:db/cas 17592186256599 :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be the event that the selected student is engaged in a sports section, and let $B$ be the event that the student is a boy. Then ${B}^{′}$ is the event that the student is a girl.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected student is engaged in a sports section, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(S)=P(S\\cap B)+P(S\\cap {B}^{′})\n$$\n\nThere are $20$ boys in the class, and $5%$ of them are engaged in a sports section. So, the number of boys engaged in a sports section is\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{100}\\cdot 20=1.\n$$\n\nThere are $24$  girls in the class, and $25%$ of them are engaged in a sports section. So, the number of girls  engaged in a sports section is\n\n$$\n\\frac{25}{100}\\cdot 24=6.\n$$\n\nTo calculate $P(S\\cap B),$ we take the number of boys engaged in a sports section $(1)$ and divide it by the total number of students in the class $(20+24=44).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(S\\cap B)=\\frac{1}{44}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to calculate $P(S\\cap {B}^{′}),$ we take the number of girls engaged in a sports section $(6)$ and divide it by the total number of students in the class $(44).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(S\\cap {B}^{′})=\\frac{6}{44}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected student is engaged in a sports section is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(S) & =P(S\\cap B)+P(S\\cap {B}^{′}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{44}+\\frac{6}{44} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{44}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $S$ be the event that the selected student is engaged in a sports section, and let $B$ be the event that the student is a boy. Then ${B}^{′}$ is the event that the student is a girl.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected student is engaged in a sports section, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(S)=P(S\\cap B)+P(S\\cap {B}^{′})\n$$\n\nThere are $20$ boys in the class, and $5\\%$ o