gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-7$ |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nNow, we work out the $y$ values using $y=t+1.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-7$ |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nPlotting these points, we obtain the following curve:\n\n![](images/e0/e06724560817f82e97c4606754a704cef47832ed89ab458dc3072622ce71d158.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186259565 :question/worked-solution "We need to create a table of values and then sketch the curve. First, we work out the $x$-values using the formula $x=2\\sqrt{t}.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ | $2.83$ | $3.46$ | $4$ |\n| $y$ | $$ | $$ | $$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nNow, we work out the $y$ values using $y=t.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ | $2.83$ | $3.46$ | $4$ |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nPlotting these points, we obtain the following curve:\n\n![](images/89/898075d0f74bdfcbab28e50ee327770400f6f58d82728cd8bf55cc75e852d161.png)" "We need to create a table of values and then sketch the curve. First, we work out the $x$-values using the formula $x=2\\sqrt{t}.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ | $2.83$ | $3.46$ | $4$ |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nNow, we work out the $y$ values using $y=t.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ | $2.83$ | $3.46$ | $4$ |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nPlotting these points, we obtain the following curve:\n\n![](images/89/898075d0f74bdfcbab28e50ee327770400f6f58d82728cd8bf55cc75e852d161.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186262584 :question/worked-solution "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A *continuous* random variable is a random variable whose possible values are represented by an interval of real numbers.\n\nCommon examples of continuous random variables are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/05/05e3d893f6c069dce5b51dc7d9fa5830b44bca58fa071009548fde20ebe92c19.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement “The percentage of impurity in a batch of chemicals”  describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real interval $[0,100]$ (since the percentage can be anywhere between $0%$ and $100%$).\n\n- Statement “The area of an equilateral triangle with a side of $2$” does *not* describe a continuous random variable. The area of an equilateral triangle with a given side length is not the outcome of a trial or experiment.\n\n- Statement “The amount of snowfall, in centimeters, in a randomly selected city in January 2022” describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real interval $[0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The percentage of impurity in a batch of chemicals”; “The amount of snowfall, in centimeters, in a randomly selected city in January 2022” only." "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A *continuous* random variable is a random variable whose possible values are represented by an interval of real numbers.\n\nCommon examples of continuous random variables are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/05/05e3d893f6c069dce5b51dc7d9fa5830b44bca58fa071009548fde20ebe92c19.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement “The percentage of impurity in a batch of chemicals”  describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real interval $[0,100]$ (since the percentage can be anywhere between $0\\%$ and $100\\%$).\n\n- Statement “The area of an equilateral triangle with a side of $2$” does *not* describe a continuous random variable. The area of an equilateral triangle with a given side length is not the outcome of a trial or experiment.\n\n- Statement “The amount of snowfall, in centimeters, in a randomly selected city in January 2022” describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real interval $[0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The percentage of impurity in a batch of chemicals”; “The amount of snowfall, in centimeters, in a randomly selected city in January 2022” only."] [:db/cas 17592186262800 :question/problem "Calculate $det(C)$, if\n    $$\n\\displaystyle C=\\begin{bmatrix}-x & -2y \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & 5y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" "Calculate $det(C)$, if\n$$\n\\displaystyle C=\\begin{bmatrix}-x & -2y \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & 5y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262802 :question/problem "Calculate $det(D)$, if\n    $$\n\\displaystyle D=\\begin{bmatrix}2x & y \\\\ -5 & xy\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" "Calculate $det(D)$, if\n$$\n\\displaystyle D=\\begin{bmatrix}2x & y \\\\ -5 & xy\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262803 :question/problem "If\n    $$\n\\displaystyle U=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ 2k & -k+2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand\n$\\text{det}(U)=-1$, what is the value of $k?$" "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle U=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ 2k & -k+2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand\n$\\text{det}(U)=-1$, what is the value of $k?$"] [:db/cas 17592186265899 :question/worked-solution "A binomial distribution models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (where each outcome is either success or failure).\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable “The number of students who like their teacher among $200$ randomly selected primary school children” could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of children who like their teacher among $200$ randomly selected students can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $200$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable “The number of three-point shots made by a basketball player in 15 shots” could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of three-point shots in $15$ shots can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $15$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable “The number of purchases made with a credit card in a store out of $10$ observed purchases, if, on average, $75%$ of all purchases are made with a credit card” could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of purchases made with a credit card in $10$ purchases can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $10$ independent experiments.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The number of students who like their teacher among $200$ randomly selected primary school children”; “The number of three-point shots made by a basketball player in 15 shots”; “The number of purchases made with a credit card in a store out of $10$ observed purchases, if, on average, $75%$ of all purchases are made with a credit card”." "A binomial distribution models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (where each outcome is either success or failure).\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable “The number of students who like their teacher among $200$ randomly selected primary school children” could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of children who like their teacher among $200$ randomly selected students can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $200$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable “The number of three-point shots made by a basketball player in 15 shots” could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of three-point shots in $15$ shots can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $15$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable “The number of purchases made with a credit card in a store out of $10$ observed purchases, if, on average, $75\\%$ of all purchases are made with a credit card” could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of purchases made with a credit card in $10$ purchases can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $10$ independent experiments.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The number of students who like their teacher among $200$ randomly selected primary school children”; “The number of three-point shots made by a basketball player in 15 shots”; “The number of purchases made with a credit card in a store out of $10$ observed purchases, if, on average, $75\\%$ of all purchases are made with a credit card”."] [:db/cas 17592186265952 :question/problem "At an ice cream shop, on average, $25%$ of customers buy chocolate ice cream. What is the probability that the $4$th customer of the day is the first person buying a chocolate ice cream? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." "At an ice cream shop, on average, $25\\%$ of customers buy chocolate ice cream. What is the probability that the $4$th customer of the day is the first person buying a chocolate ice cream? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186265952 :question/worked-solution "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of customers that enter the ice cream shop until the first one buys a chocolate ice cream. Since the chance of a customer buying a chocolate ice cream is $25%,$ the number of customers before the first buys a chocolate ice cream can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=25%=\\frac{25}{100}=0.25.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.25).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.25)^{x-1}(0.25) \\\\  & =(0.75)^{x-1}(0.25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the $4$th customer of the day is the first buying a chocolate ice cream is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=4) & =f(4) \\\\  & =(0.75)^{4-1}(0.25) \\\\  & =(0.75)^{3}(0.25) \\\\  & ≈0.105\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of customers that enter the ice cream shop until the first one buys a chocolate ice cream. Since the chance of a customer buying a chocolate ice cream is $25\\%,$ the number of customers before the first buys a chocolate ice cream can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=25\\%=\\frac{25}{100}=0.25.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.25).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.25)^{x-1}(0.25) \\\\  & =(0.75)^{x-1}(0.25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the $4$th customer of the day is the first buying a chocolate ice cream is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=4) & =f(4) \\\\  & =(0.75)^{4-1}(0.25) \\\\  & =(0.75)^{3}(0.25) \\\\  & ≈0.105\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186265969 :question/problem "In a certain school, $17.5%$ of students are left-handed. In a random sample of $8$ students from this school, what is the probability that at least $6$ students are left-handed? Round your answer to $4$ decimal places." "In a certain school, $17.5\\%$ of students are left-handed. In a random sample of $8$ students from this school, what is the probability that at least $6$ students are left-handed? Round your answer to $4$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186265977 :question/problem "A coin is being randomly picked from a jar and put back in until getting a one-dollar coin. The jar holds a $70%$ of one-dollar coins. What is the probability that a one-dollar coin is chosen before the $5$th trial?  Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." "A coin is being randomly picked from a jar and put back in until getting a one-dollar coin. The jar holds a $70\\%$ of one-dollar coins. What is the probability that a one-dollar coin is chosen before the $5$th trial?  Round your answer to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186265977 :question/worked-solution "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of choices before choosing a one-dollar coin. Since the chance of choosing a one-dollar coin is $70%,$ the number of choices required to choose a one-dollar coin can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=70%=\\frac{70}{100}=0.7.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.7).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.7)^{x-1}(0.7) \\\\  & =(0.3)^{x-1}(0.7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of choosing a one-dollar coin before the $5$th trial is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<5) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3,4}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4) \\\\  & =(0.3)^{1-1}(0.7)+(0.3)^{2-1}(0.7)+(0.3)^{3-1}(0.7)+(0.3)^{4-1}(0.7) \\\\  & =(0.7)+(0.3)(0.7)+(0.3)^{2}(0.7)+(0.3)^{3}(0.7) \\\\  & =[1+0.3+(0.3)^{2}+(0.3)^{3}](0.7) \\\\  & ≈0.992\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of choices before choosing a one-dollar coin. Since the chance of choosing a one-dollar coin is $70\\%,$ the number of choices required to choose a one-dollar coin can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=70\\%=\\frac{70}{100}=0.7.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.7).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.7)^{x-1}(0.7) \\\\  & =(0.3)^{x-1}(0.7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of choosing a one-dollar coin before the $5$th trial is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<5) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3,4}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4) \\\\  & =(0.3)^{1-1}(0.7)+(0.3)^{2-1}(0.7)+(0.3)^{3-1}(0.7)+(0.3)^{4-1}(0.7) \\\\  & =(0.7)+(0.3)(0.7)+(0.3)^{2}(0.7)+(0.3)^{3}(0.7) \\\\  & =[1+0.3+(0.3)^{2}+(0.3)^{3}](0.7) \\\\  & ≈0.992\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186265999 :question/problem "A basketball player has a $70%$ success rate in shooting from the free throw line during a season. The player has ten shots from the free throw line in one match. What is the probability that the player is successful with at most eight throws? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." "A basketball player has a $70\\%$ success rate in shooting from the free throw line during a season. The player has ten shots from the free throw line in one match. What is the probability that the player is successful with at most eight throws? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186265999 :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be the number of successful free throws.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=10$ shots from the free throw line in the match\n\n- the probability that the player is successful from the free throw line is $p=70%=0.7$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(10,0.7).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.7)^{x}(1-0.7)^{10-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.7)^{x}(0.3)^{10-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that the player is successful with at most eight throws. This corresponds to $P(X\\le 8).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\le 8)=P(X=0)+P(X=1)+…+P(X=8)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 8) & =1-P(X>8) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=9)+P(X=10)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{9})(0.7)^{9}(0.3)^{10-9}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{10})(0.7)^{10}(0.3)^{10-10}] \\\\  & =1-[10(0.7)^{9}(0.3)^{1}+1(0.7)^{10}(0.3)^{0}] \\\\  & =1-0.149\\,308 \\\\  & ≈0.851\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "Let the random variable $X$ be the number of successful free throws.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=10$ shots from the free throw line in the match\n\n- the probability that the player is successful from the free throw line is $p=70\\%=0.7$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(10,0.7).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.7)^{x}(1-0.7)^{10-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.7)^{x}(0.3)^{10-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that the player is successful with at most eight throws. This corresponds to $P(X\\le 8).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\le 8)=P(X=0)+P(X=1)+…+P(X=8)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 8) & =1-P(X>8) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=9)+P(X=10)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{9})(0.7)^{9}(0.3)^{10-9}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{10})(0.7)^{10}(0.3)^{10-10}] \\\\  & =1-[10(0.7)^{9}(0.3)^{1}+1(0.7)^{10}(0.3)^{0}] \\\\  & =1-0.149\\,308 \\\\  & ≈0.851\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186268917 :question/problem "A cinema admission fee is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ dollars and a standard deviation of $$0.75.$ Additionally, in $94%$ of cases the admission fee is between $$0.7\\mu $ and $$1.3\\mu .$ Find the percentage of cases when the admission fee is lower than $$5.$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.75\\,$ | $\\,0.80\\,$ | $\\,0.85\\,$ | $\\,0.90\\,$ | $\\,0.95\\,$ | $\\,0.97\\,$ | $\\,0.99\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.8808\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6554\\,$ | $\\,0.6591\\,$ | $\\,0.6628\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.6915\\,$ | $\\,0.6950\\,$ | $\\,0.6985\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7257\\,$ | $\\,0.7291\\,$ | $\\,0.7324\\,$ |" "A cinema admission fee is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ dollars and a standard deviation of $\\$0.75.$ Additionally, in $94\\%$ of cases the admission fee is between $\\$0.7\\mu $ and $\\$1.3\\mu .$ Find the percentage of cases when the admission fee is lower than $\\$5.$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.75\\,$ | $\\,0.80\\,$ | $\\,0.85\\,$ | $\\,0.90\\,$ | $\\,0.95\\,$ | $\\,0.97\\,$ | $\\,0.99\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.8808\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6554\\,$ | $\\,0.6591\\,$ | $\\,0.6628\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.6915\\,$ | $\\,0.6950\\,$ | $\\,0.6985\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7257\\,$ | $\\,0.7291\\,$ | $\\,0.7324\\,$ |"] [:db/cas 17592186268917 :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent a randomly selected admission fee. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{0.75}^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X<5).$ But first, we need to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(0.7\\mu <X<1.3\\mu )=0.94.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.7\\mu <X<1.3\\mu ) & =0.94 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<1.3\\mu ) & =0.94 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<1.3\\mu ) & =0.47 \\\\ P(X<\\mu )+P(\\mu <X<1.3\\mu ) & =0.5+0.47 \\\\ P(X<1.3\\mu ) & =0.97\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.3\\mu ) & =0.97 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{0.3\\mu }{0.75}) & =0.97 \\\\ Φ\\left(\\frac{0.3\\mu }{0.75}\\right) & =0.97 \\\\ \\frac{0.3\\mu }{0.75} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.97) \\\\ 0.4\\mu  & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.97).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.97)≈1.8808.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.4\\mu  & =1.8808 \\\\ \\mu  & =\\frac{1.8808}{0.4} \\\\  & ≈4.702.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X<5)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<5) & =P(Z<\\frac{5-4.702.}{0.75}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<0.40) \\\\  & =Φ(0.40) \\\\  & =0.6554 \\\\  & ≈65.5%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately in $65.5%$ of cases, the admission fee is lower than $$5.$" "Let $X$ represent a randomly selected admission fee. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{0.75}^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X<5).$ But first, we need to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(0.7\\mu <X<1.3\\mu )=0.94.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.7\\mu <X<1.3\\mu ) & =0.94 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<1.3\\mu ) & =0.94 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<1.3\\mu ) & =0.47 \\\\ P(X<\\mu )+P(\\mu <X<1.3\\mu ) & =0.5+0.47 \\\\ P(X<1.3\\mu ) & =0.97\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.3\\mu ) & =0.97 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{0.3\\mu }{0.75}) & =0.97 \\\\ Φ\\left(\\frac{0.3\\mu }{0.75}\\right) & =0.97 \\\\ \\frac{0.3\\mu }{0.75} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.97) \\\\ 0.4\\mu  & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.97).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.97)≈1.8808.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.4\\mu  & =1.8808 \\\\ \\mu  & =\\frac{1.8808}{0.4} \\\\  & ≈4.702.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X<5)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<5) & =P(Z<\\frac{5-4.702.}{0.75}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<0.40) \\\\  & =Φ(0.40) \\\\  & =0.6554 \\\\  & ≈65.5\\%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately in $65.5\\%$ of cases, the admission fee is lower than $\\$5.$"] [:db/cas 17592186268925 :question/problem "The diameter of the kibble of a particular cat food brand is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ and a standard deviation of $0.7\\,\\text{mm}.$ Additionally, the diameter of $20%$ of kibbles is less than $4.4\\,\\text{mm}$ in diameter. Find the percentage of kibbles whose diameter is less than $4\\,\\text{mm},$ rounded to $1$ decimal place.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.15\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.4\\,$ | $\\,0.0808\\,$ | $\\,0.0793\\,$ | $\\,0.0778\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.0968\\,$ | $\\,0.0951\\,$ | $\\,0.0934\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.1151\\,$ | $\\,0.1131\\,$ | $\\,0.1112\\,$ |" "The diameter of the kibble of a particular cat food brand is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ and a standard deviation of $0.7\\,\\text{mm}.$ Additionally, the diameter of $20\\%$ of kibbles is less than $4.4\\,\\text{mm}$ in diameter. Find the percentage of kibbles whose diameter is less than $4\\,\\text{mm},$ rounded to $1$ decimal place.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.15\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.4\\,$ | $\\,0.0808\\,$ | $\\,0.0793\\,$ | $\\,0.0778\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.0968\\,$ | $\\,0.0951\\,$ | $\\,0.0934\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.1151\\,$ | $\\,0.1131\\,$ | $\\,0.1112\\,$ |"] [:db/cas 17592186268925 :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the diameter of a randomly selected kibble. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{0.7}^{2}),$ and we want to find the $P(X<4).$\n\nWe know that $P(X<4.4)=0.20.$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<4.4) & =0.20 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{4.4-\\mu }{0.7}) & =0.20 \\\\ Φ\\left(\\frac{4.4-\\mu }{0.7}\\right) & =0.20 \\\\ \\frac{4.4-\\mu }{0.7} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.20)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.20)≈-0.8416.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4.4-\\mu }{0.7} & =-0.8416 \\\\ 4.4-\\mu  & =-0.58912 \\\\ \\mu  & =4.98912\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X<4)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<4) & =P(X\\le 4) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le \\frac{4-4.98912}{0.7}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\le -1.41) \\\\  & =0.0793 \\\\  & ≈7.9%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $7.9%$ of kibbles are less than $4\\,\\text{mm}$ in diameter." "Let $X$ represent the diameter of a randomly selected kibble. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{0.7}^{2}),$ and we want to find the $P(X<4).$\n\nWe know that $P(X<4.4)=0.20.$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<4.4) & =0.20 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{4.4-\\mu }{0.7}) & =0.20 \\\\ Φ\\left(\\frac{4.4-\\mu }{0.7}\\right) & =0.20 \\\\ \\frac{4.4-\\mu }{0.7} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.20)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.20)≈-0.8416.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4.4-\\mu }{0.7} & =-0.8416 \\\\ 4.4-\\mu  & =-0.58912 \\\\ \\mu  & =4.98912\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X<4)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<4) & =P(X\\le 4) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le \\frac{4-4.98912}{0.7}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\le -1.41) \\\\  & =0.0793 \\\\  & ≈7.9\\%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $7.9\\%$ of kibbles are less than $4\\,\\text{mm}$ in diameter."] [:db/cas 17592186268935 :question/problem "![](images/91/91e15117e7797cfce1b94c33d157c2e89fad519c80c715bfc3ef8082c1e38946.png)\n\nThe scatter plot shows the value of a car $y,$ in thousands of dollars, and the number of years $x$ since it was purchased. The equation of the trend line is $y=28-x.$ \n\nAssuming this trend continues, estimate the time it will take for the car to be worth $$20\\,000.$" "![](images/91/91e15117e7797cfce1b94c33d157c2e89fad519c80c715bfc3ef8082c1e38946.png)\n\nThe scatter plot shows the value of a car $y,$ in thousands of dollars, and the number of years $x$ since it was purchased. The equation of the trend line is $y=28-x.$ \n\nAssuming this trend continues, estimate the time it will take for the car to be worth $\\$20\\,000.$"] [:db/cas 17592186268935 :question/worked-solution "To predict the number of years it will take for the car to be worth $$20\\,000,$ we need to substitute $y=20$ into the equation of the trend line and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =28-x \\\\ 20 & =28-x \\\\ 20+x & =28 \\\\ x & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, according to this model, it will take approximately $8$ years for the car to be worth $$20\\,000.$\n\nNote: The point where $y=20$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable." "To predict the number of years it will take for the car to be worth $\\$20\\,000,$ we need to substitute $y=20$ into the equation of the trend line and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =28-x \\\\ 20 & =28-x \\\\ 20+x & =28 \\\\ x & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, according to this model, it will take approximately $8$ years for the car to be worth $\\$20\\,000.$\n\nNote: The point where $y=20$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable."] [:db/cas 17592186268941 :question/worked-solution "First, using the hint, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot.\n\n![](images/14/145a6a57bcd24c99e7de73f470de61c673b10f5a0d88b0c64527a17e52a0dc86.png)\n\nThe trend line passes through $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(2,5)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(4,4).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-5}{4-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)}{2} \\\\  & =-0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=-0.5$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=-0.5x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can do this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(2,5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-0.5x+b \\\\ 5 & =-0.5(2)+b \\\\ 5 & =-1+b \\\\ b & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=-0.5x+6.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=10$ into the equation above, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-0.5x+6 \\\\  & =-0.5(10)+6 \\\\  & =-5+6 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, the motorcycle's value $10$ years after it was purchased is around $$1\\,000.$\n\nNote: The point where $x=10$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable." "First, using the hint, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot.\n\n![](images/14/145a6a57bcd24c99e7de73f470de61c673b10f5a0d88b0c64527a17e52a0dc86.png)\n\nThe trend line passes through $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(2,5)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(4,4).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-5}{4-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)}{2} \\\\  & =-0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=-0.5$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=-0.5x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can do this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(2,5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-0.5x+b \\\\ 5 & =-0.5(2)+b \\\\ 5 & =-1+b \\\\ b & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=-0.5x+6.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=10$ into the equation above, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-0.5x+6 \\\\  & =-0.5(10)+6 \\\\  & =-5+6 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, the motorcycle's value $10$ years after it was purchased is around $\\$1\\,000.$\n\nNote: The point where $x=10$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable."] [:db/cas 17592186268961 :question/worked-solution "![](images/39/39e7370f9cf517a7a139fecf71561041a9b1470150e0fa333d3fff679f77882d.png)\n\n    \n\nTo determine the overall percentage obtained by the student who studied $7$ hours each week, we locate $7$ on the horizontal axis and move in the vertical direction until we find some points, as shown above.\n\nThe highlighted point has coordinates $(7,85).$ Therefore, the student who studied $7$ hours each week obtained an overall percentage of $85%.$" "![](images/39/39e7370f9cf517a7a139fecf71561041a9b1470150e0fa333d3fff679f77882d.png)\n\n    \n\nTo determine the overall percentage obtained by the student who studied $7$ hours each week, we locate $7$ on the horizontal axis and move in the vertical direction until we find some points, as shown above.\n\nThe highlighted point has coordinates $(7,85).$ Therefore, the student who studied $7$ hours each week obtained an overall percentage of $85\\%.$"] [:db/cas 17592186268964 :question/worked-solution "![](images/50/500b2e9395d0af1b9ae86ce6e9b874b817690b61faad548c1fa9e33d495a5776.png)\n\nTo determine the overall math score of the student who watches TV for $10$ hours, we locate $10$ on the horizontal axis and move in the vertical direction until we find some points, as shown above.\n\nThe highlighted point has coordinates $(10,75).$ Therefore, the overall math score of the student who watches TV for $10$ hours per week is $75%.$" "![](images/50/500b2e9395d0af1b9ae86ce6e9b874b817690b61faad548c1fa9e33d495a5776.png)\n\nTo determine the overall math score of the student who watches TV for $10$ hours, we locate $10$ on the horizontal axis and move in the vertical direction until we find some points, as shown above.\n\nThe highlighted point has coordinates $(10,75).$ Therefore, the overall math score of the student who watches TV for $10$ hours per week is $75\\%.$"] [:db/cas 17592186268989 :question/problem "| **Temperature, in ${}^{∘}C$ ($x$)** | $24$ | $26$ | $24$ | $22$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Lemonade Sales, in $$$ ($y$)** | $180$ | $202$ | $190$ | $140$ |\n\nThe owner of a lemonade stand keeps track of how much they sell in relation to the daily temperature. The table above shows the data they collected over the past four days. Compute the covariance of the data sets." "| **Temperature, in ${}^{∘}C$ ($x$)** | $24$ | $26$ | $24$ | $22$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Lemonade Sales, in \\$ ($y$)** | $180$ | $202$ | $190$ | $140$ |\n\nThe owner of a lemonade stand keeps track of how much they sell in relation to the daily temperature. The table above shows the data they collected over the past four days. Compute the covariance of the data sets."] [:db/cas 17592186268996 :question/worked-solution "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{1}{2!}n(n-1){x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{6} & =1+6x+\\frac{1}{2!}\\cdot 6\\cdot 5\\cdot {x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n} \\\\  & =1+6x+15{x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(1+x)}^{6}≈1+6x+15{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=-0.1$ into the above approximation of $(1+x)^{6}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0.9)^{6} & =(1+(-0.1))^{6} \\\\  & ≈1+6(-0.1)+15(-0.1)^{2} \\\\  & =1-6(0.1)+15(0.01) \\\\  & =1-0.6+0.15 \\\\  & =0.55\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using a calculator, we can compute the percentage error, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}%\\,error & =\\frac{\\text{amount of error}}{\\text{exact value}}\\times 100 \\\\  & =\\frac{|(0.9)^{6}-0.55|}{(0.9)^{6}}\\times 100 \\\\  & ≈3.49%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{1}{2!}n(n-1){x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{6} & =1+6x+\\frac{1}{2!}\\cdot 6\\cdot 5\\cdot {x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n} \\\\  & =1+6x+15{x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(1+x)}^{6}≈1+6x+15{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=-0.1$ into the above approximation of $(1+x)^{6}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0.9)^{6} & =(1+(-0.1))^{6} \\\\  & ≈1+6(-0.1)+15(-0.1)^{2} \\\\  & =1-6(0.1)+15(0.01) \\\\  & =1-0.6+0.15 \\\\  & =0.55\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using a calculator, we can compute the percentage error, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}%\\,error & =\\frac{\\text{amount of error}}{\\text{exact value}}\\times 100 \\\\  & =\\frac{|(0.9)^{6}-0.55|}{(0.9)^{6}}\\times 100 \\\\  & ≈3.49\\%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186268997 :question/worked-solution "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{1}{2!}n(n-1){x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{7} & =1+7x+\\frac{1}{2!}\\cdot 7\\cdot 6\\cdot {x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n} \\\\  & =1+7x+21{x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(1+x)}^{7}≈1+7x+21{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=0.02$ into the above approximation of $(1+x)^{7}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1.02)^{7} & =(1+0.02)^{7} \\\\  & ≈1+7(0.02)+21(0.02)^{2} \\\\  & =1+7(0.02)+21(0.000\\,4) \\\\  & =1+0.14+0.008\\,4 \\\\  & =1.148\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using a calculator, we can compute the percentage error, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}%\\,error & =\\frac{\\text{amount of error}}{\\text{exact value}}\\times 100 \\\\  & =\\frac{|(1.02)^{7}-1.148\\,4|}{(1.02)^{7}}\\times 100 \\\\  & ≈0.02%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{1}{2!}n(n-1){x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{7} & =1+7x+\\frac{1}{2!}\\cdot 7\\cdot 6\\cdot {x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n} \\\\  & =1+7x+21{x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(1+x)}^{7}≈1+7x+21{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=0.02$ into the above approximation of $(1+x)^{7}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1.02)^{7} & =(1+0.02)^{7} \\\\  & ≈1+7(0.02)+21(0.02)^{2} \\\\  & =1+7(0.02)+21(0.000\\,4) \\\\  & =1+0.14+0.008\\,4 \\\\  & =1.148\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using a calculator, we can compute the percentage error, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}%\\,error & =\\frac{\\text{amount of error}}{\\text{exact value}}\\times 100 \\\\  & =\\frac{|(1.02)^{7}-1.148\\,4|}{(1.02)^{7}}\\times 100 \\\\  & ≈0.02\\%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186268998 :question/worked-solution "The binomial theorem for positive integer $n$ states that\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $a=2,$ $b=x,$ and $n=5,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(2+x)}^{5} & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0}){(2)}^{5}{(x)}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1}){(2)}^{5-1}{(x)}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2}){(2)}^{5-2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1{(2)}^{5}+5{(2)}^{4}{(x)}^{1}+10{(2)}^{3}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1(32)+5(16)(x)+10(8)({x}^{2})+⋯ \\\\  & =32+80x+80{x}^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(2+x)}^{5}≈32+80x+80{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=0.1$ into the above approximation of $(2+x)^{5}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2.1)^{5} & =(2+0.1)^{5} \\\\  & ≈32+80(0.1)+80(0.1)^{2} \\\\  & =32+80(0.1)+80(0.01) \\\\  & =32+8+0.8 \\\\  & =40.8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using a calculator, we can compute the percentage error, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}%\\,error & =\\frac{\\text{amount of error}}{\\text{exact value}}\\times 100 \\\\  & =\\frac{|(2.1)^{5}-40.8|}{(2.1)^{5}}\\times 100 \\\\  & ≈0.10%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." "The binomial theorem for positive integer $n$ states that\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $a=2,$ $b=x,$ and $n=5,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(2+x)}^{5} & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0}){(2)}^{5}{(x)}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1}){(2)}^{5-1}{(x)}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2}){(2)}^{5-2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1{(2)}^{5}+5{(2)}^{4}{(x)}^{1}+10{(2)}^{3}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1(32)+5(16)(x)+10(8)({x}^{2})+⋯ \\\\  & =32+80x+80{x}^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(2+x)}^{5}≈32+80x+80{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=0.1$ into the above approximation of $(2+x)^{5}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2.1)^{5} & =(2+0.1)^{5} \\\\  & ≈32+80(0.1)+80(0.1)^{2} \\\\  & =32+80(0.1)+80(0.01) \\\\  & =32+8+0.8 \\\\  & =40.8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using a calculator, we can compute the percentage error, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}%\\,error & =\\frac{\\text{amount of error}}{\\text{exact value}}\\times 100 \\\\  & =\\frac{|(2.1)^{5}-40.8|}{(2.1)^{5}}\\times 100 \\\\  & ≈0.10\\%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186268999 :question/worked-solution "The binomial theorem for positive integer $n$ states that\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $a=2,$ $b=x,$ and $n=5,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(2+x)}^{5} & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0}){(2)}^{5}{(x)}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1}){(2)}^{5-1}{(x)}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2}){(2)}^{5-2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1{(2)}^{5}+5{(2)}^{4}{(x)}^{1}+10{(2)}^{3}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1(32)+5(16)(x)+10(8)({x}^{2})+⋯ \\\\  & =32+80x+80{x}^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(2+x)}^{5}≈32+80x+80{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=-0.02$ into the above approximation of $(2+x)^{5}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1.98)^{5} & =(2+(-0.02))^{5} \\\\  & ≈32+80(-0.02)+80(-0.02)^{2} \\\\  & =32-80(0.02)+80(0.000\\,4) \\\\  & =32-1.6+0.032 \\\\  & =30.432\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using a calculator, we can compute the percentage error, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}%\\,error & =\\frac{\\text{amount of error}}{\\text{exact value}}\\times 100 \\\\  & =\\frac{|(1.98)^{5}-30.432|}{(1.98)^{5}}\\times 100 \\\\  & ≈0.0%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." "The binomial theorem for positive integer $n$ states that\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $a=2,$ $b=x,$ and $n=5,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(2+x)}^{5} & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0}){(2)}^{5}{(x)}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1}){(2)}^{5-1}{(x)}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2}){(2)}^{5-2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1{(2)}^{5}+5{(2)}^{4}{(x)}^{1}+10{(2)}^{3}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1(32)+5(16)(x)+10(8)({x}^{2})+⋯ \\\\  & =32+80x+80{x}^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(2+x)}^{5}≈32+80x+80{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=-0.02$ into the above approximation of $(2+x)^{5}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1.98)^{5} & =(2+(-0.02))^{5} \\\\  & ≈32+80(-0.02)+80(-0.02)^{2} \\\\  & =32-80(0.02)+80(0.000\\,4) \\\\  & =32-1.6+0.032 \\\\  & =30.432\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using a calculator, we can compute the percentage error, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}%\\,error & =\\frac{\\text{amount of error}}{\\text{exact value}}\\times 100 \\\\  & =\\frac{|(1.98)^{5}-30.432|}{(1.98)^{5}}\\times 100 \\\\  & ≈0.0\\%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."] [:db/cas 17592186269000 :question/worked-solution "The binomial theorem for positive integer $n$ states that\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $a=3,$ $b=x,$ and $n=5,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(3+x)}^{5} & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0}){(3)}^{5}{(x)}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1}){(3)}^{5-1}{(x)}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2}){(3)}^{5-2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1{(3)}^{5}+5{(3)}^{4}{(x)}^{1}+10{(3)}^{3}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1(243)+5(81)(x)+10(27)({x}^{2})+⋯ \\\\  & =243+405x+270{x}^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(3+x)}^{5}≈243+405x+270{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=-0.08$ into the above approximation of $(3+x)^{5}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2.92)^{5} & =(3+(-0.08))^{5} \\\\  & ≈243+405(-0.08)+270(-0.08)^{2} \\\\  & =243-405(0.08)+270(0.006\\,4) \\\\  & =243-32.4+1.728 \\\\  & =212.328\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using a calculator, we can compute the percentage error, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}%\\,error & =\\frac{\\text{amount of error}}{\\text{exact value}}\\times 100 \\\\  & =\\frac{|(2.92)^{5}-212.328|}{(2.92)^{5}}\\times 100 \\\\  & ≈0.02%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." "The binomial theorem for positive integer $n$ states that\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $a=3,$ $b=x,$ and $n=5,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(3+x)}^{5} & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0}){(3)}^{5}{(x)}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1}){(3)}^{5-1}{(x)}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2}){(3)}^{5-2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1{(3)}^{5}+5{(3)}^{4}{(x)}^{1}+10{(3)}^{3}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1(243)+5(81)(x)+10(27)({x}^{2})+⋯ \\\\  & =243+405x+270{x}^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(3+x)}^{5}≈243+405x+270{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=-0.08$ into the above approximation of $(3+x)^{5}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2.92)^{5} & =(3+(-0.08))^{5} \\\\  & ≈243+405(-0.08)+270(-0.08)^{2} \\\\  & =243-405(0.08)+270(0.006\\,4) \\\\  & =243-32.4+1.728 \\\\  & =212.328\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using a calculator, we can compute the percentage error, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}%\\,error & =\\frac{\\text{amount of error}}{\\text{exact value}}\\times 100 \\\\  & =\\frac{|(2.92)^{5}-212.328|}{(2.92)^{5}}\\times 100 \\\\  & ≈0.02\\%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186271966 :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 8\\end{bmatrix}}^{X}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 8.5 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 6.5\\end{bmatrix}}^{y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}38 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}44 \\\\ 250\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}17.2 \\\\ -1.4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=17.2-1.4x.$\n\nFinally, to predict the selling price of a car of the same model that is $10$ years old, we substitute $x=10$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =17.2-1.4(10) \\\\  & =3.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model, the sale price will be $$3\\,200.$\n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=10$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable." "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 8\\end{bmatrix}}^{X}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 8.5 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 6.5\\end{bmatrix}}^{y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}38 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}44 \\\\ 250\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}17.2 \\\\ -1.4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=17.2-1.4x.$\n\nFinally, to predict the selling price of a car of the same model that is $10$ years old, we substitute $x=10$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =17.2-1.4(10) \\\\  & =3.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model, the sale price will be $\\$3\\,200.$\n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=10$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable."] [:db/cas 17592186275200 :question/problem "A local newspaper is sold by three distributors, $1,$ $2,$ and $3.$\n\n- Distributor $1$ gets $36%$ of the copies.\n\n- Distributor $2$ gets $23%$ of the copies, and $92%$ of those copies are sold.\n\n- Distributor $3$ gets $41%$ of the copies, and $95%$ of those copies are sold.\n\nIf $3%$ of all copies have some printing defect, $70%$ of defective copies are sold, and $2.5%$ of sold copies are defective, what is the probability that a randomly chosen copy bought by distributor $1$ is sold? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." "A local newspaper is sold by three distributors, $1,$ $2,$ and $3.$\n\n- Distributor $1$ gets $36\\%$ of the copies.\n\n- Distributor $2$ gets $23\\%$ of the copies, and $92\\%$ of those copies are sold.\n\n- Distributor $3$ gets $41\\%$ of the copies, and $95\\%$ of those copies are sold.\n\nIf $3\\%$ of all copies have some printing defect, $70\\%$ of defective copies are sold, and $2.5\\%$ of sold copies are defective, what is the probability that a randomly chosen copy bought by distributor $1$ is sold? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186275200 :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be the event that a copy has a printing defect, $S$ be the event that a copy is sold, and ${D}_{1},{D}_{2},{D}_{3}$ be the event that a copy is given to distributor $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively.\n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({D}_{1})=0.36 \\\\  & P({D}_{2})=0.23 &  & P(S|{D}_{2})=0.92 \\\\  & P({D}_{3})=0.41 &  & P(S|{D}_{3})=0.95\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${D}_{1}⊔{D}_{2}⊔{D}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({D}_{1})+P({D}_{2})+P({D}_{3})=0.36+0.23+0.41=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $3%$ of all copies has some printing defect, we have\n\n$$\nP(R)=0.03.\n$$\n\n- Since $70%$ of defective copies are sold, we have\n\n$$\nP(S|R)=0.7.\n$$\n\n- Since $2.5%$ of sold copies are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(R|S)=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen copy bought by distributor $1$ is sold, which is represented as $P(S|{D}_{1}).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R|S) & =\\frac{P(S|R)P(R)}{P(S)} \\\\ P(R|S) & =\\frac{P(S|R)P(R)}{P(S|{D}_{1})P({D}_{1})+P(S|{D}_{2})P({D}_{2})+P(S|{D}_{3})P({D}_{3})} \\\\ 0.025 & =\\frac{(0.7)(0.03)}{P(S|{D}_{1})(0.36)+(0.92)(0.23)+(0.95)(0.41)} \\\\ 0.025 & =\\frac{0.021}{0.36P(S|{D}_{1})+0.6011}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(S|{D}_{1}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.025(0.36P(S|{D}_{1})+0.6011) & =0.021 \\\\ 0.36P(S|{D}_{1})+0.6011 & =\\frac{0.021}{0.025} \\\\ 0.36P(S|{D}_{1})+0.6011 & =\\frac{21}{25} \\\\ 0.36P(S|{D}_{1}) & =\\frac{21}{25}-0.6011 \\\\ P(S|{D}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{0.36}(\\frac{21}{25}-0.6011) \\\\ P(S|{D}_{1}) & ≈0.664\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." "Let $R$ be the event that a copy has a printing defect, $S$ be the event that a copy is sold, and ${D}_{1},{D}_{2},{D}_{3}$ be the event that a copy is given to distributor $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively.\n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({D}_{1})=0.36 \\\\  & P({D}_{2})=0.23 &  & P(S|{D}_{2})=0.92 \\\\  & P({D}_{3})=0.41 &  & P(S|{D}_{3})=0.95\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${D}_{1}⊔{D}_{2}⊔{D}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({D}_{1})+P({D}_{2})+P({D}_{3})=0.36+0.23+0.41=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $3\\%$ of all copies has some printing defect, we have\n\n$$\nP(R)=0.03.\n$$\n\n- Since $70\\%$ of defective copies are sold, we have\n\n$$\nP(S|R)=0.7.\n$$\n\n- Since $2.5\\%$ of sold copies are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(R|S)=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen copy bought by distributor $1$ is sold, which is represented as $P(S|{D}_{1}).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R|S) & =\\frac{P(S|R)P(R)}{P(S)} \\\\ P(R|S) & =\\frac{P(S|R)P(R)}{P(S|{D}_{1})P({D}_{1})+P(S|{D}_{2})P({D}_{2})+P(S|{D}_{3})P({D}_{3})} \\\\ 0.025 & =\\frac{(0.7)(0.03)}{P(S|{D}_{1})(0.36)+(0.92)(0.23)+(0.95)(0.41)} \\\\ 0.025 & =\\frac{0.021}{0.36P(S|{D}_{1})+0.6011}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(S|{D}_{1}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.025(0.36P(S|{D}_{1})+0.6011) & =0.021 \\\\ 0.36P(S|{D}_{1})+0.6011 & =\\frac{0.021}{0.025} \\\\ 0.36P(S|{D}_{1})+0.6011 & =\\frac{21}{25} \\\\ 0.36P(S|{D}_{1}) & =\\frac{21}{25}-0.6011 \\\\ P(S|{D}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{0.36}(\\frac{21}{25}-0.6011) \\\\ P(S|{D}_{1}) & ≈0.664\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186275277 :question/problem "At an archaeological site, an object is found with $30%$ of its original carbon-14 content. Given that the half-life of carbon-14 is $5\\,730$ years, approximately how old is the object?" "At an archaeological site, an object is found with $30\\%$ of its original carbon-14 content. Given that the half-life of carbon-14 is $5\\,730$ years, approximately how old is the object?"] [:db/cas 17592186281298 :question/worked-solution "We need to create a table of values and then sketch the curve. First, we work out the $x$-values using the formula $x=2t.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $0.5$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ | $10$ |\n| $y$ | $$ | $$ | $$ | $$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nNow, we work out the $y$ values using $y=\\frac{5}{t}.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $0.5$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ | $10$ |\n| $y$ | $10$ | $5$ | $2.5$ | $1.67$ | $1.25$ | $1$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nPlotting th