he division.$$\n\n| $-6{x}^{4}+25{x}^{3}-28{x}^{2}+x+13$ | __ | {{field-1}} | $+$ | {{field-2}} |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+x+4$ | __ |  |  | $3{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+x+4$ |" "Express $\\frac{-6{x}^{4}+25{x}^{3}-28{x}^{2}+x+13}{3{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+x+4}$ in the form $q(x)+\\frac{r(x)}{3{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+x+4},$ where $q(x)$ is the quotient and $r(x)$ is the remainder of the division.\n\n| $-6{x}^{4}+25{x}^{3}-28{x}^{2}+x+13$ | __ | {{field-1}} | $+$ | {{field-2}} |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+x+4$ | __ |  |  | $3{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+x+4$ |"] [:db/cas 17592186302180 :question/problem "The manufacturing times of bicycle frames are normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ minutes and a standard deviation of $5$ minutes. Additionally, $25%$ of frames are completed in under $48$ minutes. Find the percentage of frames that take longer than $52$ minutes. **Round your answer to two decimal places.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.5517\\,$ | $\\,0.5557\\,$ | $\\,0.5596\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.5910\\,$ | $\\,0.5948\\,$ | $\\,0.5987\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.6293\\,$ | $\\,0.6331\\,$ | $\\,0.6368\\,$ |\n\nApproximately\n{{field-1}}$%$ of the frames take longer than $52$ minutes." "The manufacturing times of bicycle frames are normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ minutes and a standard deviation of $5$ minutes. Additionally, $25\\%$ of frames are completed in under $48$ minutes. Find the percentage of frames that take longer than $52$ minutes. **Round your answer to two decimal places.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.5517\\,$ | $\\,0.5557\\,$ | $\\,0.5596\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.5910\\,$ | $\\,0.5948\\,$ | $\\,0.5987\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.6293\\,$ | $\\,0.6331\\,$ | $\\,0.6368\\,$ |\n\nApproximately\n{{field-1}}$%$ of the frames take longer than $52$ minutes."] [:db/cas 17592186302180 :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the time required to manufacture a bicycle frame. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{5}^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X>52).$ \n\nWe know that $P(X<48)=0.25.$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<48) & =0.25 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{48-\\mu }{5}) & =0.25 \\\\ Φ\\left(\\frac{48-\\mu }{5}\\right) & =0.25 \\\\ \\frac{48-\\mu }{5} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.25)=-0.6745.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{48-\\mu }{5} & =-0.6745 \\\\ 48-\\mu  & =-3.3725 \\\\ \\mu  & =51.3725.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(52<X)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>52) & =P(Z>\\frac{52-51.3725}{5}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>0.13) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.13) \\\\  & =1-0.5517 \\\\  & ≈44.83%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $44.83%$ of the frames take longer than $52$ minutes." "Let $X$ represent the time required to manufacture a bicycle frame. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{5}^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X>52).$ \n\nWe know that $P(X<48)=0.25.$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<48) & =0.25 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{48-\\mu }{5}) & =0.25 \\\\ Φ\\left(\\frac{48-\\mu }{5}\\right) & =0.25 \\\\ \\frac{48-\\mu }{5} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.25)=-0.6745.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{48-\\mu }{5} & =-0.6745 \\\\ 48-\\mu  & =-3.3725 \\\\ \\mu  & =51.3725.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(52<X)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>52) & =P(Z>\\frac{52-51.3725}{5}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>0.13) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.13) \\\\  & =1-0.5517 \\\\  & ≈44.83\\%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $44.83\\%$ of the frames take longer than $52$ minutes."] [:db/cas 17592186302182 :question/problem "The resting heart rate of a group of adults is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu \\,\\text{bpm}$ and a standard deviation of $8\\,\\text{bpm}.$ Additionally, $70%$ of the adults have a resting heart rate between $0.9\\mu \\,\\text{bpm}$ and $1.1\\mu \\,\\text{bpm}.$ Find the percentage of adults with a resting heart rate lower than $70\\,\\text{bpm}.$ **Round your answer to two decimal places.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.75\\,$ | $\\,0.80\\,$ | $\\,0.85\\,$ | $\\,0.90\\,$ | $\\,0.95\\,$ | $\\,0.99\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.8\\,$ | $\\,0.0359\\,$ | $\\,0.0351\\,$ | $\\,0.0344\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.7\\,$ | $\\,0.0446\\,$ | $\\,0.0436\\,$ | $\\,0.0427\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.6\\,$ | $\\,0.0548\\,$ | $\\,0.0537\\,$ | $\\,0.0526\\,$ |\n\nApproximately\n{{field-1}}$%$ of the adults have a resting heart rate lower than $70\\,\\text{bpm}.$" "The resting heart rate of a group of adults is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu \\,\\text{bpm}$ and a standard deviation of $8\\,\\text{bpm}.$ Additionally, $70\\%$ of the adults have a resting heart rate between $0.9\\mu \\,\\text{bpm}$ and $1.1\\mu \\,\\text{bpm}.$ Find the percentage of adults with a resting heart rate lower than $70\\,\\text{bpm}.$ **Round your answer to two decimal places.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.75\\,$ | $\\,0.80\\,$ | $\\,0.85\\,$ | $\\,0.90\\,$ | $\\,0.95\\,$ | $\\,0.99\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.8\\,$ | $\\,0.0359\\,$ | $\\,0.0351\\,$ | $\\,0.0344\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.7\\,$ | $\\,0.0446\\,$ | $\\,0.0436\\,$ | $\\,0.0427\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.6\\,$ | $\\,0.0548\\,$ | $\\,0.0537\\,$ | $\\,0.0526\\,$ |\n\nApproximately\n{{field-1}}$%$ of the adults have a resting heart rate lower than $70\\,\\text{bpm}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186302182 :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the resting heart rate of a randomly selected adult. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{8}^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X<70).$ But first, we need to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(0.9\\mu <X<1.1\\mu )=0.70.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.9\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.70 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.70 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.35 \\\\ P(X<\\mu )+P(\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.5+0.35 \\\\ P(X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.85\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.85 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{0.1\\mu }{8}) & =0.85 \\\\ Φ\\left(\\frac{0.1\\mu }{8}\\right) & =0.85 \\\\ \\frac{0.1\\mu }{8} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.85).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.85)=1.0364.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.1\\mu }{8} & =1.0364 \\\\ 0.1\\mu  & =8.2912 \\\\ \\mu  & =\\frac{8.2912}{0.1} \\\\  & ≈82.912.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X<70)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<70) & =P(Z<\\frac{70-82.912}{8}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<-1.61) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.61) \\\\  & =0.0537 \\\\  & ≈5.37%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $5.37%$ of the adults have a resting heart rate lower than $70\\,\\text{bpm}.$" "Let $X$ represent the resting heart rate of a randomly selected adult. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{8}^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X<70).$ But first, we need to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(0.9\\mu <X<1.1\\mu )=0.70.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.9\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.70 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.70 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.35 \\\\ P(X<\\mu )+P(\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.5+0.35 \\\\ P(X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.85\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.85 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{0.1\\mu }{8}) & =0.85 \\\\ Φ\\left(\\frac{0.1\\mu }{8}\\right) & =0.85 \\\\ \\frac{0.1\\mu }{8} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.85).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.85)=1.0364.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.1\\mu }{8} & =1.0364 \\\\ 0.1\\mu  & =8.2912 \\\\ \\mu  & =\\frac{8.2912}{0.1} \\\\  & ≈82.912.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X<70)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<70) & =P(Z<\\frac{70-82.912}{8}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<-1.61) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.61) \\\\  & =0.0537 \\\\  & ≈5.37\\%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $5.37\\%$ of the adults have a resting heart rate lower than $70\\,\\text{bpm}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186302186 :question/problem "Express $\\frac{4{x}^{4}+3{x}^{2}+3}{\\,{x}^{2}-x+2\\,}$ in the form $q(x)+\\frac{r(x)}{\\,{x}^{2}-x+2\\,},$ where $q(x)$ is the quotient and $r(x)$ is the reminder of the division.$$\n\n| $4{x}^{4}+3{x}^{2}+3$ | __ | {{field-1}} | $+$ | {{field-2}} |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}-x+2$ | __ |  |  | ${x}^{2}-x+2$ |" "Express $\\frac{4{x}^{4}+3{x}^{2}+3}{\\,{x}^{2}-x+2\\,}$ in the form $q(x)+\\frac{r(x)}{\\,{x}^{2}-x+2\\,},$ where $q(x)$ is the quotient and $r(x)$ is the reminder of the division.\n\n| $4{x}^{4}+3{x}^{2}+3$ | __ | {{field-1}} | $+$ | {{field-2}} |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}-x+2$ | __ |  |  | ${x}^{2}-x+2$ |"] [:db/cas 17592186302187 :question/problem "Express $\\frac{2{x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+1}{\\,{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x-4\\,}$ in the form $q(x)+\\frac{r(x)}{\\,{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x-4\\,},$ where $q(x)$ is the quotient and $r(x)$ is the reminder of the division.$$\n\n| $2{x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+1$ | __ | {{field-1}} | $+$ | {{field-2}} |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x-4$ | __ |  |  | ${x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x-4$ |" "Express $\\frac{2{x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+1}{\\,{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x-4\\,}$ in the form $q(x)+\\frac{r(x)}{\\,{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x-4\\,},$ where $q(x)$ is the quotient and $r(x)$ is the reminder of the division.\n\n| $2{x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+1$ | __ | {{field-1}} | $+$ | {{field-2}} |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x-4$ | __ |  |  | ${x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x-4$ |"] [:db/cas 17592186302188 :question/problem "Express $\\frac{-9{x}^{3}+22x}{\\,-3x+4\\,}$ in the form $q(x)+\\frac{r(x)}{\\,-3x+4\\,},$ where $q(x)$ is the quotient and $r(x)$ is the reminder of the division.$$\n\n| $-9{x}^{3}+22x$ | __ | {{field-1}} | $+$ | {{field-2}} |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-3x+4$ | __ |  |  | $-3x+4$ |" "Express $\\frac{-9{x}^{3}+22x}{\\,-3x+4\\,}$ in the form $q(x)+\\frac{r(x)}{\\,-3x+4\\,},$ where $q(x)$ is the quotient and $r(x)$ is the reminder of the division.\n\n| $-9{x}^{3}+22x$ | __ | {{field-1}} | $+$ | {{field-2}} |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-3x+4$ | __ |  |  | $-3x+4$ |"] [:db/cas 17592186304915 :question/problem "A biased spinner has a $60%$ chance of landing on blue. The spinner is spun $4$ times. Rounded to three decimal places, what is the probability of getting blue exactly $2$ times?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}." "A biased spinner has a $60\\%$ chance of landing on blue. The spinner is spun $4$ times. Rounded to three decimal places, what is the probability of getting blue exactly $2$ times?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186304915 :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times the spinner landed on blue.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=4$ spins.\n\n- The probability of landing on blue is $p=60%=0.6.$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(4,0.6).$ We want to find the probability of landing on blue $x=2$ times.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=2) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2})(0.6)^{2}(1-0.6)^{4-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4!}{2!(4-2)!}(0.6)^{2}(0.4)^{2} \\\\  & =6(0.6)^{2}(0.4)^{2} \\\\  & ≈0.346,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times the spinner landed on blue.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=4$ spins.\n\n- The probability of landing on blue is $p=60\\%=0.6.$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(4,0.6).$ We want to find the probability of landing on blue $x=2$ times.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=2) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2})(0.6)^{2}(1-0.6)^{4-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4!}{2!(4-2)!}(0.6)^{2}(0.4)^{2} \\\\  & =6(0.6)^{2}(0.4)^{2} \\\\  & ≈0.346,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186304923 :question/problem "A new software program fails to run with probability $70%.$ If it is tested on $5$ computers, rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability that it fails on $1$ or fewer of them?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}." "A new software program fails to run with probability $70\\%.$ If it is tested on $5$ computers, rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability that it fails on $1$ or fewer of them?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186304923 :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of failed runs.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=5$ tests.\n\n- The probability of failure is $p=70%=0.7.$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(5,0.7).$\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x})(0.7)^{x}(1-0.7)^{5-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x})(0.7)^{x}(0.3)^{5-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that $1$ or fewer of the $5$ software program runs fail. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1) & =P(X=0)+P(X=1) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0})(0.7)^{0}(0.3)^{5}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1})(0.7)^{1}(0.3)^{4} \\\\  & =1(0.3)^{5}+5(0.7)(0.3)^{4} \\\\  & ≈0.031,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of failed runs.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=5$ tests.\n\n- The probability of failure is $p=70\\%=0.7.$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(5,0.7).$\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x})(0.7)^{x}(1-0.7)^{5-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x})(0.7)^{x}(0.3)^{5-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that $1$ or fewer of the $5$ software program runs fail. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1) & =P(X=0)+P(X=1) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0})(0.7)^{0}(0.3)^{5}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1})(0.7)^{1}(0.3)^{4} \\\\  & =1(0.3)^{5}+5(0.7)(0.3)^{4} \\\\  & ≈0.031,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186304960 :question/problem "A text message has a $20%$ chance of being flagged as spam by a filter. Suppose $10$ messages are randomly selected. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability that at least $2$ are flagged as spam?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}." "A text message has a $20\\%$ chance of being flagged as spam by a filter. Suppose $10$ messages are randomly selected. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability that at least $2$ are flagged as spam?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186304960 :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of messages flagged as spam.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=10$ messages.\n\n- The probability of a message being flagged as spam is $p=20%=0.20.$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(10,0.20).$\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.20)^{x}(1-0.20)^{10-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.20)^{x}(0.80)^{10-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability of at least $2$ messages being flagged. This corresponds to $P(X\\ge 2).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 2)=P(X=2)+P(X=3)+…+P(X=10)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =1-P(X<2) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{0})(0.20)^{0}(0.80)^{10-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{1})(0.20)^{1}(0.80)^{10-1}] \\\\  & =1-[(0.80)^{10}+10(0.20)(0.80)^{9}] \\\\  & =1-0.375\\,809… \\\\  & ≈0.624,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of messages flagged as spam.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=10$ messages.\n\n- The probability of a message being flagged as spam is $p=20\\%=0.20.$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(10,0.20).$\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.20)^{x}(1-0.20)^{10-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.20)^{x}(0.80)^{10-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability of at least $2$ messages being flagged. This corresponds to $P(X\\ge 2).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 2)=P(X=2)+P(X=3)+…+P(X=10)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =1-P(X<2) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{0})(0.20)^{0}(0.80)^{10-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{1})(0.20)^{1}(0.80)^{10-1}] \\\\  & =1-[(0.80)^{10}+10(0.20)(0.80)^{9}] \\\\  & =1-0.375\\,809… \\\\  & ≈0.624,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186305018 :question/problem "A customer service representative resolves a caller’s issue with a $45%$ chance during each minute of a call. The call continues until the issue is resolved. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability that it will take exactly $5$ minutes to resolve the issue is{{field-1}}." "A customer service representative resolves a caller’s issue with a $45\\%$ chance during each minute of a call. The call continues until the issue is resolved. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability that it will take exactly $5$ minutes to resolve the issue is{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186305018 :question/worked-solution "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of minutes until the representative resolves the issue. Since the chance of resolving the issue in any given minute is $45%,$ the number of minutes until the issue is resolved can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=45%=\\frac{45}{100}=0.45.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.45).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.45)^{x-1}(0.45) \\\\  & =(0.55)^{x-1}(0.45)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the representative will resolve the issue for the first time in the $5$th minute is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=5) & =f(5) \\\\  & =(0.55)^{5-1}(0.45) \\\\  & =(0.55)^{4}(0.45) \\\\  & ≈0.041,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of minutes until the representative resolves the issue. Since the chance of resolving the issue in any given minute is $45\\%,$ the number of minutes until the issue is resolved can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=45\\%=\\frac{45}{100}=0.45.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.45).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.45)^{x-1}(0.45) \\\\  & =(0.55)^{x-1}(0.45)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the representative will resolve the issue for the first time in the $5$th minute is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=5) & =f(5) \\\\  & =(0.55)^{5-1}(0.45) \\\\  & =(0.55)^{4}(0.45) \\\\  & ≈0.041,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186305023 :question/problem "A smartphone receives a software update that has a $60%$ chance of installing successfully on any given attempt. The phone keeps trying until the update installs successfully. Rounded to $2$ decimal places, the probability that the update installs successfully within the first $2$ attempts is{{field-1}}." "A smartphone receives a software update that has a $60\\%$ chance of installing successfully on any given attempt. The phone keeps trying until the update installs successfully. Rounded to $2$ decimal places, the probability that the update installs successfully within the first $2$ attempts is{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186305023 :question/worked-solution "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of attempts until the update installs successfully. Since the chance that the update installs successfully on a single attempt is $60%,$ the number of attempts required to install the update can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=60%=\\frac{60}{100}=0.6.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.6).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.6)^{x-1}(0.6) \\\\  & =(0.4)^{x-1}(0.6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the update installs successfully in one of the first $2$ attempts is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2) & =P(X\\in {1,2}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & =(0.4)^{1-1}(0.6)+(0.4)^{2-1}(0.6) \\\\  & =[1+(0.4)](0.6) \\\\  & =0.84.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of attempts until the update installs successfully. Since the chance that the update installs successfully on a single attempt is $60\\%,$ the number of attempts required to install the update can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=60\\%=\\frac{60}{100}=0.6.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.6).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.6)^{x-1}(0.6) \\\\  & =(0.4)^{x-1}(0.6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the update installs successfully in one of the first $2$ attempts is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2) & =P(X\\in {1,2}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & =(0.4)^{1-1}(0.6)+(0.4)^{2-1}(0.6) \\\\  & =[1+(0.4)](0.6) \\\\  & =0.84.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186305026 :question/worked-solution "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of patients tested until the first positive result is obtained. Since the chance that any single patient tests positive is $3%,$ the number of patients tested until the first positive result can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=\\frac{9}{300}=0.03.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.03).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.03)^{x-1}(0.03) \\\\  & =(0.97)^{x-1}(0.03)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(X>6),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X>6)=1-P(X\\le 6)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 6) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3,4,5,6}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4)+f(5)+f(6) \\\\  & =(0.97)^{1-1}(0.03)+(0.97)^{2-1}(0.03)+(0.97)^{3-1}(0.03) \\\\  & \\,+(0.97)^{4-1}(0.03)+(0.97)^{5-1}(0.03)+(0.97)^{6-1}(0.03) \\\\  & =[1+(0.97)+(0.97)^{2}+(0.97)^{3}+(0.97)^{4}+(0.97)^{5}](0.03) \\\\  & ≈0.167,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the probability that the nurse will test more than $6$ patients before seeing the first positive result is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>6) & =1-P(X\\le 6) \\\\  & ≈1-0.167 \\\\  & ≈0.833,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "A geometric random variable $X$ with prob