ligned}\\text{E}[p] & =1998\\cdot f(1998)+(-2)\\cdot f(-2) \\\\  & =1998\\left(\\frac{1}{10,000}\\right)-2\\left(\\frac{9,999}{10,000}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1998}{10,000}-\\frac{19,998}{10,000} \\\\  & =-\\frac{18,000}{10,000} \\\\  & =-1.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected profit is $-\\$\\,1.8.$"] [:db/cas 17592186311839 :question/problem "Suppose we play a game that costs $$2$ per round. Each player randomly selects one card from a standard $52$-card deck. If the ace of spades is selected, the player wins $$100.$ Otherwise, the player wins nothing. What is the expected profit of a player in this game?\n\nRounded to two decimal places, the expected profit is $-$$\n{{field-1}}" "Suppose we play a game that costs $\\$2$ per round. Each player randomly selects one card from a standard $52$-card deck. If the ace of spades is selected, the player wins $\\$100.$ Otherwise, the player wins nothing. What is the expected profit of a player in this game?\n\nRounded to two decimal places, the expected profit is $-$\\$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186311839 :question/worked-solution "Let $p$ represent the profit in this game. There are $52$ possible cards that can be selected. One of these cards is the winning card. In the game, there are two possible outcomes:\n\n- If the ace of spades is selected, the player wins $$100.$ This means the player’s profit is $$100-$2=$98,$ so we have $p=98.$\n\n- If the ace of spades is not selected, they lose $$2,$ so we have $p=-2.$\n\nThe probabilities for the outcomes are\n\n$$\nP(98)=\\frac{1}{52},\\,P(-2)=\\frac{51}{52}.\n$$\n\nLet's summarize this in a table. If $p$ is the profit (in dollars) on a single game, and $f(p)$ is the probability mass function for $p,$ then we have the following:\n\n| $p$ | $98$ | $-2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(p)$ | $\\frac{1}{52}$ | $\\frac{51}{52}$ |\n\nTherefore, the expected value of $p$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[p] & =98\\cdot f(98)+(-2)\\cdot f(-2) \\\\  & =98\\left(\\frac{1}{52}\\right)-2\\left(\\frac{51}{52}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{98}{52}-\\frac{102}{52} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{13} \\\\  & ≈-0.08.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected profit is approximately $-$\\,0.08.$" "Let $p$ represent the profit in this game. There are $52$ possible cards that can be selected. One of these cards is the winning card. In the game, there are two possible outcomes:\n\n- If the ace of spades is selected, the player wins $\\$100.$ This means the player’s profit is $\\$100-$2=\\$98,$ so we have $p=98.$\n\n- If the ace of spades is not selected, they lose $$2,$ so we have $p=-2.$\n\nThe probabilities for the outcomes are\n\n$$\nP(98)=\\frac{1}{52},\\,P(-2)=\\frac{51}{52}.\n$$\n\nLet's summarize this in a table. If $p$ is the profit (in dollars) on a single game, and $f(p)$ is the probability mass function for $p,$ then we have the following:\n\n| $p$ | $98$ | $-2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(p)$ | $\\frac{1}{52}$ | $\\frac{51}{52}$ |\n\nTherefore, the expected value of $p$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[p] & =98\\cdot f(98)+(-2)\\cdot f(-2) \\\\  & =98\\left(\\frac{1}{52}\\right)-2\\left(\\frac{51}{52}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{98}{52}-\\frac{102}{52} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{13} \\\\  & ≈-0.08.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected profit is approximately $-\\$\\,0.08.$"] [:db/cas 17592186311840 :question/problem "Consider a game of chance with $4$ prizes ($$1$, $$2$, $$3$, and $$5$) where each outcome has a specific probability of happening, shown in the table below. \n\n| $\\text{Number}$ | $\\text{Probability}$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $$1$ | $25%$ |\n| $$2$ | $25%$ |\n| $$3$ | $30%$ |\n| $$5$ | $20%$ |\n\nWhat is the expected profit per game?\n\nThe expected profit is $$$\n{{field-1}}" "Consider a game of chance with $4$ prizes ($\\$1$, $\\$2$, $\\$3$, and $\\$5$) where each outcome has a specific probability of happening, shown in the table below. \n\n| $\\text{Number}$ | $\\text{Probability}$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\$1$ | $25\\%$ |\n| $\\$2$ | $25\\%$ |\n| $\\$3$ | $30\\%$ |\n| $\\$5$ | $20\\%$ |\n\nWhat is the expected profit per game?\n\nThe expected profit is \\$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186311840 :question/worked-solution "Let $p$ represent the profit on a single game, in dollars. Using the given probabilities, let's summarize this in a table. If $f(p)$ is the probability mass function for $p,$ then we have the following:\n\n| $f(p)$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{3}{10}$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $p$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $5$ |\n\nTherefore, the expected value of $p$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[p] & =1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3)+5\\cdot f(5) \\\\  & =1\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)+2\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)+3\\left(\\frac{3}{10}\\right)+5\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{9}{10}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{5+10+18+20}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{53}{20} \\\\  & =2.65.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected profit is $$\\,2.65.$" "Let $p$ represent the profit on a single game, in dollars. Using the given probabilities, let's summarize this in a table. If $f(p)$ is the probability mass function for $p,$ then we have the following:\n\n| $f(p)$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{3}{10}$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $p$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $5$ |\n\nTherefore, the expected value of $p$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[p] & =1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3)+5\\cdot f(5) \\\\  & =1\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)+2\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)+3\\left(\\frac{3}{10}\\right)+5\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{9}{10}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{5+10+18+20}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{53}{20} \\\\  & =2.65.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected profit is $\\$\\,2.65.$"] [:db/cas 17592186311841 :question/problem "Consider a game of chance with $5$ prizes ($$0$, $$2$, $$3$, $$5$, and $$10$) where each outcome has a specific probability of happening, shown in the table below. \n\n| $\\text{Number}$ | $\\text{Probability}$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $$0$ | $10%$ |\n| $$2$ | $25%$ |\n| $$3$ | $30%$ |\n| $$5$ | $20%$ |\n| $$10$ | $15%$ |\n\nWhat is the expected profit per game?\n\nThe expected profit is $$$\n{{field-1}}" "Consider a game of chance with $5$ prizes ($\\$0$, $\\$2$, $\\$3$, $\\$5$, and $\\$10$) where each outcome has a specific probability of happening, shown in the table below. \n\n| $\\text{Number}$ | $\\text{Probability}$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\$0$ | $10\\%$ |\n| $\\$2$ | $25\\%$ |\n| $\\$3$ | $30\\%$ |\n| $\\$5$ | $20\\%$ |\n| $\\$10$ | $15\\%$ |\n\nWhat is the expected profit per game?\n\nThe expected profit is \\$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186311841 :question/worked-solution "Let $p$ represent the profit on a single game, in dollars. Using the given probabilities, let's summarize this in a table.  If $f(p)$ is the probability mass function for $p,$ then we have the following:\n\n| $f(p)$ | $\\frac{1}{10}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{3}{10}$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $\\frac{3}{20}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $p$ | $0$ | $2$ | $3$ | $5$ | $10$ |\n\nTherefore, the expected value of $p$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[p] & =0\\cdot f(0)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3)+5\\cdot f(5)+10\\cdot f(10) \\\\  & =0\\left(\\frac{1}{10}\\right)+2\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)+3\\left(\\frac{3}{10}\\right)+5\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)+10\\left(\\frac{3}{20}\\right) \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{9}{10}+1+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{10}+\\frac{9}{10}+\\frac{10}{10}+\\frac{15}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{39}{10} \\\\  & =3.9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected profit is $$\\,3.9.$" "Let $p$ represent the profit on a single game, in dollars. Using the given probabilities, let's summarize this in a table.  If $f(p)$ is the probability mass function for $p,$ then we have the following:\n\n| $f(p)$ | $\\frac{1}{10}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{3}{10}$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $\\frac{3}{20}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $p$ | $0$ | $2$ | $3$ | $5$ | $10$ |\n\nTherefore, the expected value of $p$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[p] & =0\\cdot f(0)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3)+5\\cdot f(5)+10\\cdot f(10) \\\\  & =0\\left(\\frac{1}{10}\\right)+2\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)+3\\left(\\frac{3}{10}\\right)+5\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)+10\\left(\\frac{3}{20}\\right) \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{9}{10}+1+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{10}+\\frac{9}{10}+\\frac{10}{10}+\\frac{15}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{39}{10} \\\\  & =3.9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected profit is $\\$\\,3.9.$"] [:db/cas 17592186314761 :question/problem "The value $P(t)$ of a particular luxury car $t$ years after the year $2010$ can be modeled by the differential equation ${P}^{′}(t)=0.025P(t).$ If the car's value in $2016$ was $$62\\,000,$ calculate its value in the year $2025.$" "The value $P(t)$ of a particular luxury car $t$ years after the year $2010$ can be modeled by the differential equation ${P}^{′}(t)=0.025P(t).$ If the car's value in $2016$ was $\\$62\\,000,$ calculate its value in the year $2025.$"] [:db/cas 17592186314761 :question/worked-solution "The general solution to the given differential equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)={P}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.025t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{0}$ is the initial value of the car.\n\nWe are given that the value in $2016$ was $$62\\,000.$ So, substituting $t=6$ and $P(6)=$62\\,000$ into our general solution and solving for ${P}_{0},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(6) & ={P}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.025\\cdot 6} \\\\ 62\\,000 & ={P}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.15} \\\\ {P}_{0} & =\\frac{62\\,000}{{e}^{0.15}} \\\\  & ≈53\\,363.895,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nHence, our particular solution is\n\n$$\nP(t)=53\\,363.895\\,{e}^{0.025t}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the car's value $15$ years after $2010.$ So, substituting $t=15$ into our particular solution, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(15) & =53\\,363.895\\,{e}^{0.025\\cdot 15} \\\\  & =53\\,363.895\\,{e}^{0.375} \\\\  & ≈77\\,644,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest whole number. \n\nTherefore, the car's value in $2025$ will be $$77\\,644.$" "The general solution to the given differential equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)={P}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.025t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{0}$ is the initial value of the car.\n\nWe are given that the value in $2016$ was $\\$62\\,000.$ So, substituting $t=6$ and $P(6)=$62\\,000$ into our general solution and solving for ${P}_{0},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(6) & ={P}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.025\\cdot 6} \\\\ 62\\,000 & ={P}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.15} \\\\ {P}_{0} & =\\frac{62\\,000}{{e}^{0.15}} \\\\  & ≈53\\,363.895,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nHence, our particular solution is\n\n$$\nP(t)=53\\,363.895\\,{e}^{0.025t}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the car's value $15$ years after $2010.$ So, substituting $t=15$ into our particular solution, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(15) & =53\\,363.895\\,{e}^{0.025\\cdot 15} \\\\  & =53\\,363.895\\,{e}^{0.375} \\\\  & ≈77\\,644,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest whole number. \n\nTherefore, the car's value in $2025$ will be $$77\\,644.$"] [:db/cas 17592186314810 :question/worked-solution "We model the population of the bacterial culture using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=kN.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nN(t)={N}_{0}\\,{e}^{kt},\n$$\n\nwhere ${N}_{0}$ is the initial population.\n\nWe're told that the culture initially contained $500$ bacteria, so we have ${N}_{0}=500.$\n\nTherefore, the population $N$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nN(t)=500\\,{e}^{kt}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $k,$ we substitute $N(3)=2\\,000$ into the equation above and solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(3) & =500\\,{e}^{k\\cdot 3} \\\\ 2\\,000 & =500\\,{e}^{3k} \\\\ {e}^{3k} & =\\frac{2\\,000}{500} \\\\ {e}^{3k} & =4 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{3k}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4) \\\\ 3k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4) \\\\ k & =0.462\\,09…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k≈46%,$ rounded to the nearest percent." "We model the population of the bacterial culture using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=kN.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nN(t)={N}_{0}\\,{e}^{kt},\n$$\n\nwhere ${N}_{0}$ is the initial population.\n\nWe're told that the culture initially contained $500$ bacteria, so we have ${N}_{0}=500.$\n\nTherefore, the population $N$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nN(t)=500\\,{e}^{kt}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $k,$ we substitute $N(3)=2\\,000$ into the equation above and solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(3) & =500\\,{e}^{k\\cdot 3} \\\\ 2\\,000 & =500\\,{e}^{3k} \\\\ {e}^{3k} & =\\frac{2\\,000}{500} \\\\ {e}^{3k} & =4 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{3k}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4) \\\\ 3k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4) \\\\ k & =0.462\\,09…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k≈46\\%,$ rounded to the nearest percent."] [:db/cas 17592186314811 :question/problem "The value of a savings account $A=A(t)$ after $t$ years can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=kA,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is the growth rate. If the account initially had $$12\\,000,$ and $A(4)=$15\\,000,$ calculate the growth rate $k$ to the nearest percent." "The value of a savings account $A=A(t)$ after $t$ years can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=kA,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is the growth rate. If the account initially had $\\$12\\,000,$ and $A(4)=\\$15\\,000,$ calculate the growth rate $k$ to the nearest percent."] [:db/cas 17592186314811 :question/worked-solution "We model the value of the savings account using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=kA.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nA(t)={A}_{0}\\,{e}^{kt},\n$$\n\nwhere ${A}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that the account initially had $$12\\,000,$ so we have ${A}_{0}=12\\,000.$\n\nTherefore, the value $A$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nA(t)=12\\,000\\,{e}^{kt}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $k,$ we substitute $A(4)=15\\,000$ into the equation above and solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(4) & =12\\,000\\,{e}^{k\\cdot 4} \\\\ 15\\,000 & =12\\,000\\,{e}^{4k} \\\\ {e}^{4k} & =\\frac{15\\,000}{12\\,000} \\\\ {e}^{4k} & =1.25 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{4k}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.25) \\\\ 4k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.25) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.25) \\\\ k & =0.055\\,78…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k≈6%,$ rounded to the nearest percent." "We model the value of the savings account using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=kA.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nA(t)={A}_{0}\\,{e}^{kt},\n$$\n\nwhere ${A}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that the account initially had $\\$12\\,000,$ so we have ${A}_{0}=12\\,000.$\n\nTherefore, the value $A$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nA(t)=12\\,000\\,{e}^{kt}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $k,$ we substitute $A(4)=15\\,000$ into the equation above and solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(4) & =12\\,000\\,{e}^{k\\cdot 4} \\\\ 15\\,000 & =12\\,000\\,{e}^{4k} \\\\ {e}^{4k} & =\\frac{15\\,000}{12\\,000} \\\\ {e}^{4k} & =1.25 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{4k}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.25) \\\\ 4k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.25) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.25) \\\\ k & =0.055\\,78…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k≈6\\%,$ rounded to the nearest percent."] [:db/cas 17592186314971 :question/problem "The rate at which the price of a particular product changes $P(t),$ after $t$ years, is given by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=0.042P.\n$$\n\nIf the price today is $$120,$ how many years will it take for the price to increase to $$750?$\nRound your answer to the nearest year." "The rate at which the price of a particular product changes $P(t),$ after $t$ years, is given by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=0.042P.\n$$\n\nIf the price today is $\\$120,$ how many years will it take for the price to increase to $\\$750?$\nRound your answer to the nearest year."] [:db/cas 17592186314971 :question/worked-solution "We model the price growth using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=0.042P.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)={P}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.042t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that the price today is $$120,$ so we have ${P}_{0}=120.$\n\nTherefore, the price $P$ of the product after $t$ years is\n\n$$\nP(t)=120\\,{e}^{0.042t}\\,.\n$$\n\nTo find how many years it takes for the price to grow to $$750,$ we substitute $P(t)=750$ into the equation above and solve for $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$$\n\\displaystyle \n\\begin{aligned}750 & =120\\,{e}^{0.042t} \\\\ \\frac{750}{120} & ={e}^{0.042t} \\\\ {e}^{0.042t} & =\\frac{25}{4} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{0.042t}) & =ln\\,\\left(\\frac{25}{4}\\right) \\\\ 0.042t & =ln\\,\\left(\\frac{25}{4}\\right) \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{0.042}ln\\,\\left(\\frac{25}{4}\\right) \\\\ t & ≈43.633\\end{aligned}\n\n$$$\n\nRounding to the nearest integer, we conclude that it takes $44$ years for the price to increase to $$750.$" "We model the price growth using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=0.042P.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)={P}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.042t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that the price today is $\\$120,$ so we have ${P}_{0}=120.$\n\nTherefore, the price $P$ of the product after $t$ years is\n\n$$\nP(t)=120\\,{e}^{0.042t}\\,.\n$$\n\nTo find how many years it takes for the price to grow to $\\$750,$ we substitute $P(t)=750$ into the equation above and solve for $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \n\\begin{aligned}750 & =120\\,{e}^{0.042t} \\\\ \\frac{750}{120} & ={e}^{0.042t} \\\\ {e}^{0.042t} & =\\frac{25}{4} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{0.042t}) & =ln\\,\\left(\\frac{25}{4}\\right) \\\\ 0.042t & =ln\\,\\left(\\frac{25}{4}\\right) \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{0.042}ln\\,\\left(\\frac{25}{4}\\right) \\\\ t & ≈43.633\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nRounding to the nearest integer, we conclude that it takes $44$ years for the price to increase to $\\$750.$"] [:db/cas 17592186314984 :question/problem "The rate at which the cultivated area of a particular region grows $A(t),$ after $t$ years, can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=0.08A.\n$$\n\nHow many years does it take for the cultivated area to increase by $40%?$ Round your answer to the nearest decimal place." "The rate at which the cultivated area of a particular region grows $A(t),$ after $t$ years, can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=0.08A.\n$$\n\nHow many years does it take for the cultivated area to increase by $40\\%?$ Round your answer to the nearest decimal place."] [:db/cas 17592186314984 :question/worked-solution "We model the growth of the cultivated area using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=0.08A.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nA(t)={A}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.08t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${A}_{0}$ is the initial cultivated area.\n\nLet ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the cultivated area has increased by $40%.$ That is,\n\n$$\nA({t}_{1})=1.4{A}_{0}.\n$$\n\nWe solve the above equation for ${t}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A({t}_{1}) & ={A}_{0}{e}^{0.08\\cdot {t}_{1}} \\\\ 1.4{A}_{0} & ={A}_{0}{e}^{0.08{t}_{1}} \\\\ 1.4 & ={e}^{0.08{t}_{1}} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.4) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{0.08{t}_{1}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.4) & =0.08{t}_{1} \\\\ {t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.4)}{0.08} \\\\ {t}_{1} & ≈4.205\\,9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding to the nearest decimal place, we conclude that it takes $4.2$ years for the cultivated area to increase by $40%.$" "We model the growth of the cultivated area using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=0.08A.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nA(t)={A}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.08t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${A}_{0}$ is the initial cultivated area.\n\nLet ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the cultivated area has increased by $40\\%.$ That is,\n\n$$\nA({t}_{1})=1.4{A}_{0}.\n$$\n\nWe solve the above equation for ${t}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A({t}_{1}) & ={A}_{0}{e}^{0.08\\cdot {t}_{1}} \\\\ 1.4{A}_{0} & ={A}_{0}{e}^{0.08{t}_{1}} \\\\ 1.4 & ={e}^{0.08{t}_{1}} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.4) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{0.08{t}_{1}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.4) & =0.08{t}_{1} \\\\ {t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.4)}{0.08} \\\\ {t}_{1} & ≈4.205\\,9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding to the nearest decimal place, we conclude that it takes $4.2$ years for the cultivated area to increase by $40\\%.$"] [:db/cas 17592186315026 :question/problem "A balloon is being deflated. The rate at which the diameter of the balloon decays $D(t),$ after $t$ seconds, can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}=-0.20D.\n$$\n\nHow long does it take for the balloon’s diameter to reduce to $15%$ of its original diameter?  Round your answer to the nearest second." "A balloon is being deflated. The rate at which the diameter of the balloon decays $D(t),$ after $t$ seconds, can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}=-0.20D.\n$$\n\nHow long does it take for the balloon’s diameter to reduce to $15\\%$ of its original diameter?  Round your answer to the nearest second."] [:db/cas 17592186315026 :question/worked-solution "We model the diameter of the balloon using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}=-0.20D.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nD(t)={D}_{0}\\,{e}^{-0.20t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${D}_{0}$ is the initial diameter.\n\nLet ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the diameter of the balloon is $15%$ of its original diameter. That is,\n\n$$\nD({t}_{1})=0.15{D}_{0}.\n$$\n\nWe solve the above equation for ${t}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.15{D}_{0} & ={D}_{0}\\,{e}^{-0.20{t}_{1}} \\\\ 0.15 & ={e}^{-0.20{t}_{1}} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.15) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-0.20{t}_{1}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.15) & =-0.20{t}_{1} \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.15)}{0.20} \\\\ {t}_{1} & ≈9.486\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding to the nearest second, we conclude that it takes $9$ seconds for the balloon’s diameter to be $15%$ of its original diameter." "We model the diameter of the balloon using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}=-0.20D.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nD(t)={D}_{0}\\,{e}^{-0.20t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${D}_{0}$ is the initial diameter.\n\nLet ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the diameter of the balloon is $15\\%$ of its original diameter. That is,\n\n$$\nD({t}_{1})=0.15{D}_{0}.\n$$\n\nWe solve the above equation for ${t}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.15{D}_{0} & ={D}_{0}\\,{e}^{-0.20{t}_{1}} \\\\ 0.15 & ={e}^{-0.20{t}_{1}} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.15) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-0.20{t}_{1}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.15) & =-0.20{t}_{1} \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.15)}{0.20} \\\\ {t}_{1} & ≈9.486\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding to the nearest second, we conclude that it takes $9$ seconds for the balloon’s diameter to be $15\\%$ of its original diameter."] [:db/cas 17592186315027 :question/problem "The amount of charge stored in a battery is decreasing due to self-discharge. The rate at which the amount of stored charge decays $W(t),$ after $t$ months, can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}W}{\\text{d}t}=-0.025W.\n$$\n\nHow long does it take for the amount of stored charge to be $75%$ of the original amount? Round your answer to the nearest month." "The amount of charge stored in a battery is decreasing due to self-discharge. The rate at which the amount of stored charge decays $W(t),$ after $t$ months, can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}W}{\\text{d}t}=-0.025W.\n$$\n\nHow long does it take for the amount of stored charge to be $75\\%$ of the original amount? Round your answer to the nearest month."] [:db/cas 17592186315027 :question/worked-solution "We model the amount of stored charge using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}W}{\\text{d}t}=-0.025W.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nW(t)={W}_{0}\\,{e}^{-0.025t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${W}_{0}$ is the initial stored charge.\n\nLet ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the amount of stored charge is $75%$ of the original amount. That is,\n\n$$\nW({t}_{1})=0.75{W}_{0}.\n$$\n\nWe solve the above equation for ${t}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.75{W}_{0} & ={W}_{0}\\,{e}^{-0.025{t}_{1}} \\\\ 0.75 & ={e}^{-0.025{t}_{1}} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.75) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-0.025{t}_{1}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.75) & =-0.025{t}_{1} \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.75)}{0.025} \\\\ {t}_{1} & ≈11.507\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding to the nearest month, we conclude that it takes $12$ months for the amount of stored charge to be $75%$ of the original amount." "We model the amount of stored charge using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}W}{\\text{d}t}=-0.025W.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nW(t)={W}_{0}\\,{e}^{-0.025t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${W}_{0}$ is the initial stored charge.\n\nLet ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the amount of stored charge is $75\\%$ of the original amount. That is,\n\n$$\nW({t}_{1})=0.75{W}_{0}.\n$$\n\nWe solve the above equation for ${t}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.75{W}_{0} & ={W}_{0}\\,{e}^{-0.025{t}_{1}} \\\\ 0.75 & ={e}^{-0.025{t}_{1}} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.75) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-0.025{t}_{1}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.75) & =-0.025{t}_{1} \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.75)}{0.025} \\\\ {t}_{1} & ≈11.507\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding to the nearest month, we conclude that it takes $12$ months for the amount of stored charge to be $75\\%$ of the original amount."] [:db/cas 17592186317426 :question/problem "$$\n\nConsider the curve $x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=2.$ Given that $x=x(t),\\text{ }y=y(t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=6$ on the curve at the point $(4,\\frac{\\pi }{3}),$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point. Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Consider the curve $x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=2.$ Given that $x=x(t),\\text{ }y=y(t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=6$ on the curve at the point $(4,\\frac{\\pi }{3}),$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point. Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186317428 :question/problem "$$\n\nConsider the curve $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+y=0.$ Given that $x=x(t),\\text{ }y=y(t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=5$ on the curve at the point $(1,0),$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Consider the curve $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+y=0.$ Given that $x=x(t),\\text{ }y=y(t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=5$ on the curve at the point $(1,0),$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186319109 :question/worked-solution "$$\n\nNote that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{20}{25-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{4}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{x}{5})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u+C,\n$$\n\nthe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${\\left(\\frac{x}{5}\\right)}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{5}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{5}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=5\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{3}{5}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{20}{25-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{4}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{x}{5})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{3/5}\\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\cdot 5\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{3/5}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{1-{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u\\,{|}_{0}^{3/5} \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{3}{5})-4\\operatorname{artanh}⁡0 \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{3}{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{20}{25-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{4}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{x}{5})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u+C,\n$$\n\nthe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${\\left(\\frac{x}{5}\\right)}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{5}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{5}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=5\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{3}{5}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{20}{25-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{4}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{x}{5})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{3/5}\\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\cdot 5\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{3/5}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{1-{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u\\,{|}_{0}^{3/5} \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{3}{5})-4\\operatorname{artanh}⁡0 \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{3}{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186319207 :question/worked-solution "First, because the initial condition is $y(2)=3,$ we fill $y=3$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $3$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x-2y \\\\  & =4(2)-2(3) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $3$ | $2$ | $$ |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =2(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $3$ | $2$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ |" "First, because the initial condition is $y(2)=3,$ we fill $y=3$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $3$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x-2y \\\\  & =4(2)-2(3) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $3$ | $2$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =2(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $3$ | $2$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ |"] [:db/cas 17592186319208 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x{y}^{2}-1,\\,y(-1)=2\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method with step size $Δx=\\frac{1}{4}.$ Fill in the empty box in the column for $Δy$ in the table below using this method. Do not round the answer.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $$ | $$ | {{field-1}} |" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x{y}^{2}-1,\\,y(-1)=2\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method with step size $Δx=\\frac{1}{4}.$ Fill in the empty box in the column for $Δy$ in the table below using this method. Do not round the answer.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |"] [:db/cas 17592186319208 :question/worked-solution "First, because the initial condition is $y(-1)=2,$ we fill $y=2$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x{y}^{2}-1 \\\\  & =(-1)(2)^{2}-1 \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $-5$ | $$ |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =-5(\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $-5$ | $-\\frac{5}{4}$ |" "First, because the initial condition is $y(-1)=2,$ we fill $y=2$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x{y}^{2}-1 \\\\  & =(-1)(2)^{2}-1 \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $-5$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =-5(\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $-5$ | $-\\frac{5}{4}$ |"] [:db/cas 17592186319222 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x\\sqrt{y},\\,y(4)=4\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the second row of the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{4}?$ Do not round the answer.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $4$ | $8$ | $2$ |\n| $$ | $$ | $$ | {{field-1}} |" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x\\sqrt{y},\\,y(4)=4\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the second row of the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{4}?$ Do not round the answer.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $4$ | $8$ | $2$ |\n|  |  |  | {{field-1}} |"] [:db/cas 17592186319222 :question/worked-solution "To get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =4+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{4} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =4+2 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $4$ | $8$ | $2$ |\n| $\\frac{17}{4}$ | $6$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row), and we compute \n$Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x\\sqrt{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{6} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{4}\\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{17\\sqrt{6}}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $4$ | $8$ | $2$ |\n| $\\frac{17}{4}$ | $6$ | $\\frac{17}{4}\\sqrt{6}$ | $\\frac{17\\sqrt{6}}{16}$ |" "To get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =4+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{4} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =4+2 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $4$ | $8$ | $2$ |\n| $\\frac{17}{4}$ | $6$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row), and we compute \n$Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x\\sqrt{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{6} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{4}\\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{17\\sqrt{6}}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $4$ | $8$ | $2$ |\n| $\\frac{17}{4}$ | $6$ | $\\frac{17}{4}\\sqrt{6}$ | $\\frac{17\\sqrt{6}}{16}$ |"] [:db/cas 17592186319223 :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(6).$ The distance from $x=0$ and $x=6$ is $6,$ and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{6-0}{3}=\\frac{6}{3}=2\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=1.$ \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}+3y={0}^{2}+3(1)=3 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=3\\cdot 2=6 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+2=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+6=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $3$ | $6$ |\n| $2$ | $7$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}+3y={2}^{2}+3(7)=25 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=25\\cdot 2=50 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+2=4 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=7+50=57\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $3$ | $6$ |\n| $2$ | $7$ | $25$ | $50$ |\n| $4$ | $57$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 3.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the third row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}+3y={4}^{2}+3(57)=187 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=187\\cdot 2=374 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=4+2=6 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=57+374=431\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $3$ | $6$ |\n| $2$ | $7$ | $25$ | $50$ |\n| $4$ | $57$ | $187$ | $374$ |\n| $6$ | $431$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(6)≈431.$" "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(6).$ The distance from $x=0$ and $x=6$ is $6,$ and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{6-0}{3}=\\frac{6}{3}=2\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=1.$ \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}+3y={0}^{2}+3(1)=3 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=3\\cdot 2=6 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+2=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+6=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $3$ | $6$ |\n| $2$ | $7$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}+3y={2}^{2}+3(7)=25 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=25\\cdot 2=50 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+2=4 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=7+50=57\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $3$ | $6$ |\n| $2$ | $7$ | $25$ | $50$ |\n| $4$ | $57$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 3.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the third row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}+3y={4}^{2}+3(57)=187 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=187\\cdot 2=374 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=4+2=6 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=57+374=431\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $3$ | $6$ |\n| $2$ | $7$ | $25$ | $50$ |\n| $4$ | $57$ | $187$ | $374$ |\n| $6$ | $431$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(6)≈431.$"] [:db/cas 17592186319361 :question/problem "The value $P(t)$ of a painting is given by the function\n\n$$\nP(t)=250+{\\int }_{0}^{t}({3}^{x/4}-27)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ is measured in dollars, and $t>0$ is the time (in years) since the painting was created. \n\nAt what time is the value a minimum, and how much does the painting cost at this time, rounded to the nearest dollar?\n\nThe minimum value is\n$$$\n{{field-1}}\nafter\n{{field-2}}\nyears." "The value $P(t)$ of a painting is given by the function\n\n$$\nP(t)=250+{\\int }_{0}^{t}({3}^{x/4}-27)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ is measured in dollars, and $t>0$ is the time (in years) since the painting was created. \n\nAt what time is the value a minimum, and how much does the painting cost at this time, rounded to the nearest dollar?\n\nThe minimum value is\n\n{{field-1}}\nafter\n{{field-2}}\nyears."] [:db/cas 17592186319361 :question/worked-solution "To find when the value is minimized, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=0.$ First, we differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(250+{\\int }_{0}^{t}({3}^{x/4}-27)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(250)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({\\int }_{0}^{t}({3}^{x/4}-27)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}({3}^{x/4}-27)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={3}^{t/4}-27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{t/4}-27 & =0 \\\\ {3}^{t/4} & ={3}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{t}{4} & =3 \\\\ t & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo there are two candidates for global extrema, $t=0$ and $t=12.$ To find out the value of the painting when $t=12,$ we figure out $P(12):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(12) & =250+{\\int }_{0}^{12}({3}^{x/4}-27)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =250+{[\\frac{4\\cdot {3}^{x/4}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-27x]|}_{0}^{12} \\\\  & =250+[(\\frac{4\\cdot 27}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-324)-\\left(\\frac{4\\cdot 1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\right)] \\\\  & =250-324+\\frac{108-4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =-74+\\frac{104}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & ≈21,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nSince $21<250,$ we conclude that the minimum value is $$\\,21$, and this happens after $12$ years." "To find when the value is minimized, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=0.$ First, we differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(250+{\\int }_{0}^{t}({3}^{x/4}-27)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(250)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({\\int }_{0}^{t}({3}^{x/4}-27)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}({3}^{x/4}-27)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={3}^{t/4}-27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{t/4}-27 & =0 \\\\ {3}^{t/4} & ={3}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{t}{4} & =3 \\\\ t & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo there are two candidates for global extrema, $t=0$ and $t=12.$ To find out the value of the painting when $t=12,$ we figure out $P(12):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(12) & =250+{\\int }_{0}^{12}({3}^{x/4}-27)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =250+{[\\frac{4\\cdot {3}^{x/4}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-27x]|}_{0}^{12} \\\\  & =250+[(\\frac{4\\cdot 27}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-324)-\\left(\\frac{4\\cdot 1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\right)] \\\\  & =250-324+\\frac{108-4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =-74+\\frac{104}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & ≈21,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nSince $21<250,$ we conclude that the minimum value is $\\$\\,21$, and this happens after $12$ years."] [:db/cas 17592186319369 :question/worked-solution "$$\n\nNote that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{40}{64-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{5}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{x}{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u+C,\n$$\n\nthe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${\\left(\\frac{x}{8}\\right)}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{8}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{8}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=8\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{40}{64-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{5}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{x}{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{3/4}\\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\cdot 8\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{3/4}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{1-{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u\\,{|}_{0}^{3/4} \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})-5\\operatorname{artanh}⁡0 \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{40}{64-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{5}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{x}{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u+C,\n$$\n\nthe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${\\left(\\frac{x}{8}\\right)}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{8}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{8}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=8\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{40}{64-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{5}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{x}{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{3/4}\\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\cdot 8\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{3/4}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{1-{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u\\,{|}_{0}^{3/4} \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})-5\\operatorname{artanh}⁡0 \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186320884 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x\\sqrt{y},\\,y(1)=4\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the second row of the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}?$ Do not round the answer.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $4$ | $4$ | $2$ |\n| $$ | $$ | $$ | {{field-1}} |" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x\\sqrt{y},\\,y(1)=4\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the second row of the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}?$ Do not round the answer.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $4$ | $4$ | $2$ |\n|  |  |  | {{field-1}} |"] [:db/cas 17592186320884 :question/worked-solution "To get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =4+2 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $4$ | $4$ | $2$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $6$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row), and we compute \n$Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x\\sqrt{y} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{6} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{6}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $4$ | $4$ | $2$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $6$ | $3\\sqrt{6}$ | $\\frac{3\\sqrt{6}}{2}$ |" "To get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =4+2 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $4$ | $4$ | $2$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $6$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row), and we compute \n$Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x\\sqrt{y} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{6} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{6}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $4$ | $4$ | $2$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $6$ | $3\\sqrt{6}$ | $\\frac{3\\sqrt{6}}{2}$ |"] [:db/cas 17592186320885 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2y),\\,y(1)=\\frac{e}{2}\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the second row of the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}?$ Do not round the answer.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{e}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $$ | $$ | $$ | {{field-1}} |" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2y),\\,y(1)=\\frac{e}{2}\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the second row of the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}?$ Do not round the answer.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{e}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n|  |  |  | {{field-1}} |"] [:db/cas 17592186320885 :question/worked-solution "To get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{e+1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{e}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{e+1}{2}$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row), and we compute \n$Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2y) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+1) \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+1)}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{e}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{e+1}{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+1)$ | $\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+1)}{4}$ |" "To get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{e+1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{e}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{e+1}{2}$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row), and we compute \n$Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2y) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+1) \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+1)}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{e}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{e+1}{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+1)$ | $\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+1)}{4}$ |"] [:db/cas 17592186320886 :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y\\,\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ is $\\frac{3}{2},$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{(\\frac{3}{2}-0)}{2}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=1.$ \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x+2=2\\cdot 0+2=2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =1+Δy=1+\\frac{3}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{4}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n \n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x+2=2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}+2=\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=\\frac{7}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{21}{8} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{21}{8}=\\frac{41}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{4}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{7}{2}$ | $\\frac{21}{8}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{41}{8}$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, we conclude that  $$\n\\displaystyle y\\,\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)≈\\frac{41}{8}.\n$$" "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y\\,\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ is $\\frac{3}{2},$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{(\\frac{3}{2}-0)}{2}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=1.$ \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x+2=2\\cdot 0+2=2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =1+Δy=1+\\frac{3}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{4}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ |  |  |\n\n \n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x+2=2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}+2=\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=\\frac{7}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{21}{8} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{21}{8}=\\frac{41}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{4}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{7}{2}$ | $\\frac{21}{8}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{41}{8}$ |  |  |\n\n \n\nTherefore, we conclude that  $$\n\\displaystyle y\\,\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)≈\\frac{41}{8}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186320887 :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given $y(1)$ and asked to find $y(4).$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=4$ is $3,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=0.$ \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x+4=4\\cdot 1+4=8 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=8\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}=12 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{3}{2}=\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =0+Δy=0+12=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $8$ | $12$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $12$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n \n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x+4=4\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}+4=14 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=14\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}=21 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}=4 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=12+21=33\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $8$ | $12$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $12$ | $14$ | $21$ |\n| $4$ | $33$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, we conclude that  $y(4)≈33.$" "First, note that we are given $y(1)$ and asked to find $y(4).$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=4$ is $3,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=0.$ \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x+4=4\\cdot 1+4=8 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=8\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}=12 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{3}{2}=\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =0+Δy=0+12=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $8$ | $12$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $12$ |  |  |\n\n \n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x+4=4\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}+4=14 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=14\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}=21 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}=4 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=12+21=33\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $8$ | $12$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $12$ | $14$ | $21$ |\n| $4$ | $33$ |  |  |\n\n \n\nTherefore, we conclude that  $y(4)≈33.$"] [:db/cas 17592186320888 :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(1).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=1$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-0}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=2.$ \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{x}{y}=\\frac{0}{2}=0 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =2+Δy=2+0=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n \n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{x}{y}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}{2}=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+\\frac{1}{8}=\\frac{17}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $2$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{17}{8}$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, we conclude that  $y(1)≈\\frac{17}{8}.$" "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(1).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=1$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-0}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=2.$ \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{x}{y}=\\frac{0}{2}=0 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =2+Δy=2+0=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\n \n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{x}{y}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}{2}=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+\\frac{1}{8}=\\frac{17}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $2$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{17}{8}$ |  |  |\n\n \n\nTherefore, we conclude that  $y(1)≈\\frac{17}{8}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186320889 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(y+x)^{2},\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the second row of the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{4}?$ Do not round the answer.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $$ | $$ | $$ | {{field-1}} |" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(y+x)^{2},\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the second row of the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{4}?$ Do not round the answer.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n|  |  |  | {{field-1}} |"] [:db/cas 17592186320889 :question/worked-solution "To get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row), and we compute \n$Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(y+x)^{2} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{5}{4}+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $\\frac{9}{4}$ | $\\frac{9}{16}$ |" "To get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{5}{4}$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row), and we compute \n$Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(y+x)^{2} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{5}{4}+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $\\frac{9}{4}$ | $\\frac{9}{16}$ |"] [:db/cas 17592186320890 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3\\sqrt{xy},\\,y(4)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the second row of the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}?$ Do not round the answer.\n\n| 