 table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $1$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n| $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $4$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row), and we compute \n$Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3\\sqrt{xy} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{2}\\cdot 4} \\\\  & =9\\sqrt{2} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =9\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $1$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n| $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $4$ | $9\\sqrt{2}$ | $\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ |"] [:db/cas 17592186320891 :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(3).$ The distance from $x=0$ and $x=3$ is $3,$ and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-0}{3}=\\frac{3}{3}=1\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=0.$ \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={e}^{x}={e}^{0}=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot 1=1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+1=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={e}^{x}={e}^{1}=e \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=e\\cdot 1=e \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $e$ | $e$ |\n| $2$ | $1+e$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 3.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the third row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={e}^{x}={e}^{2} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx={e}^{2}\\cdot 1={e}^{2} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=(1+e)+{e}^{2}=1+e+{e}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $e$ | $e$ |\n| $2$ | $1+e$ | ${e}^{2}$ | ${e}^{2}$ |\n| $3$ | $1+e+{e}^{2}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)≈1+e+{e}^{2}.$" "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(3).$ The distance from $x=0$ and $x=3$ is $3,$ and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-0}{3}=\\frac{3}{3}=1\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=0.$ \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={e}^{x}={e}^{0}=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot 1=1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+1=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $1$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={e}^{x}={e}^{1}=e \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=e\\cdot 1=e \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $e$ | $e$ |\n| $2$ | $1+e$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 3.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the third row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={e}^{x}={e}^{2} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx={e}^{2}\\cdot 1={e}^{2} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=(1+e)+{e}^{2}=1+e+{e}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $e$ | $e$ |\n| $2$ | $1+e$ | ${e}^{2}$ | ${e}^{2}$ |\n| $3$ | $1+e+{e}^{2}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)≈1+e+{e}^{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186320894 :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given $y(-1)$ and asked to find $y(2).$ The distance from $x=-1$ and $x=2$ is $3,$ and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-(-1)}{3}=\\frac{3}{3}=1\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(-1)=2.$ \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x+5-y=-1+5-2=2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot 1=2 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=-1+1=0 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+2=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this data to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $0$ | $4$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x+5-y=0+5-4=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot 1=1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=4+1=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $0$ | $4$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $1$ | $5$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 3.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the third row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x+5-y=1+5-5=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot 1=1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=5+1=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $0$ | $4$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $1$ | $5$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $6$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈6.$" "First, note that we are given $y(-1)$ and asked to find $y(2).$ The distance from $x=-1$ and $x=2$ is $3,$ and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-(-1)}{3}=\\frac{3}{3}=1\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(-1)=2.$ \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x+5-y=-1+5-2=2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot 1=2 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=-1+1=0 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+2=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this data to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $0$ | $4$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x+5-y=0+5-4=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot 1=1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=4+1=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $0$ | $4$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $1$ | $5$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 3.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the third row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x+5-y=1+5-5=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot 1=1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=5+1=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $0$ | $4$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $1$ | $5$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $6$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈6.$"] [:db/cas 17592186320895 :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given $y(2)$ and asked to find $y(5).$ The distance from $x=2$ and $x=5$ is $3,$ and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{5-2}{3}=\\frac{3}{3}=1\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(2)=-3.$ \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $-3$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x+2y=4(2)+2(-3)=2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot 1=2 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-3+2=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $-3$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $3$ | $-1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x+2y=4(3)+2(-1)=10 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=10\\cdot 1=10 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=3+1=4 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+10=9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $-3$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $3$ | $-1$ | $10$ | $10$ |\n| $4$ | $9$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 3.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the third row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x+2y=4(4)+2(9)=34 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=34\\cdot 1=34 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=4+1=5 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=9+34=43\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $-3$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $3$ | $-1$ | $10$ | $10$ |\n| $4$ | $9$ | $34$ | $34$ |\n| $5$ | $43$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(5)≈43.$" "First, note that we are given $y(2)$ and asked to find $y(5).$ The distance from $x=2$ and $x=5$ is $3,$ and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{5-2}{3}=\\frac{3}{3}=1\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(2)=-3.$ \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $-3$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x+2y=4(2)+2(-3)=2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot 1=2 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-3+2=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $-3$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $3$ | $-1$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x+2y=4(3)+2(-1)=10 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=10\\cdot 1=10 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=3+1=4 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+10=9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $-3$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $3$ | $-1$ | $10$ | $10$ |\n| $4$ | $9$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 3.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the third row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x+2y=4(4)+2(9)=34 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=34\\cdot 1=34 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=4+1=5 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=9+34=43\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $-3$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $3$ | $-1$ | $10$ | $10$ |\n| $4$ | $9$ | $34$ | $34$ |\n| $5$ | $43$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(5)≈43.$"] [:db/cas 17592186321083 :question/worked-solution "We model the number of bacteria using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=kN.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nN(t)={N}_{0}\\,{e}^{kt},\n$$\n\nwhere ${N}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that there were $500$ bacteria initially, so we have ${N}_{0}=500.$\n\nTherefore, the number of bacteria $N$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nN(t)=500\\,{e}^{kt}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $k,$ we substitute $N(3)=900$ into the equation above and solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(3) & =500\\,{e}^{k\\cdot 3} \\\\ 900 & =500\\,{e}^{3k} \\\\ {e}^{3k} & =\\frac{900}{500} \\\\ {e}^{3k} & =\\frac{9}{5} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{3k}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{9}{5}\\right) \\\\ 3k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{9}{5}\\right) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{9}{5}\\right) \\\\ k & ≈0.1959\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k≈20%,$ rounded to the nearest percent." "We model the number of bacteria using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=kN.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nN(t)={N}_{0}\\,{e}^{kt},\n$$\n\nwhere ${N}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that there were $500$ bacteria initially, so we have ${N}_{0}=500.$\n\nTherefore, the number of bacteria $N$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nN(t)=500\\,{e}^{kt}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $k,$ we substitute $N(3)=900$ into the equation above and solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(3) & =500\\,{e}^{k\\cdot 3} \\\\ 900 & =500\\,{e}^{3k} \\\\ {e}^{3k} & =\\frac{900}{500} \\\\ {e}^{3k} & =\\frac{9}{5} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{3k}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{9}{5}\\right) \\\\ 3k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{9}{5}\\right) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{9}{5}\\right) \\\\ k & ≈0.1959\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k≈20\\%,$ rounded to the nearest percent."] [:db/cas 17592186321084 :question/worked-solution "We model the number of subscribers using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=kS.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nS(t)={S}_{0}\\,{e}^{kt},\n$$\n\nwhere ${S}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that there were $12\\,000$ subscribers initially, so we have ${S}_{0}=12\\,000.$\n\nTherefore, the number of subscribers $S$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nS(t)=12\\,000\\,{e}^{kt}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $k,$ we substitute $S(2)=15\\,000$ into the equation above and solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(2) & =12\\,000\\,{e}^{k\\cdot 2} \\\\ 15\\,000 & =12\\,000\\,{e}^{2k} \\\\ {e}^{2k} & =\\frac{15\\,000}{12\\,000} \\\\ {e}^{2k} & =\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2k}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right) \\\\ 2k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right) \\\\ k & ≈0.111\\,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k≈11%,$ rounded to the nearest percent." "We model the number of subscribers using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=kS.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nS(t)={S}_{0}\\,{e}^{kt},\n$$\n\nwhere ${S}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that there were $12\\,000$ subscribers initially, so we have ${S}_{0}=12\\,000.$\n\nTherefore, the number of subscribers $S$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nS(t)=12\\,000\\,{e}^{kt}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $k,$ we substitute $S(2)=15\\,000$ into the equation above and solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(2) & =12\\,000\\,{e}^{k\\cdot 2} \\\\ 15\\,000 & =12\\,000\\,{e}^{2k} \\\\ {e}^{2k} & =\\frac{15\\,000}{12\\,000} \\\\ {e}^{2k} & =\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2k}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right) \\\\ 2k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right) \\\\ k & ≈0.111\\,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k≈11\\%,$ rounded to the nearest percent."] [:db/cas 17592186321103 :question/worked-solution "First, because the initial condition is $y(1)=1,$ we fill $y=1$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x+2y \\\\  & =1+2(1) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $$ |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =3(\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ |" "First, because the initial condition is $y(1)=1,$ we fill $y=1$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x+2y \\\\  & =1+2(1) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $3$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =3(\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ |"] [:db/cas 17592186321104 :question/worked-solution "First, because the initial condition is $y(1)=2,$ we fill $y=2$ into the table.\n\n