40px\">\n       $0$\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"35px\">\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n       $0$\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n        {{field-2}}\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n        {{field-3}}\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n        $0$\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"35px\">\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n        $0$\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n        {{field-4}}\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n        $0$\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n        $0$\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"35px\">\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n       $0$\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n        $0$\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n        $0$\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>" "Find the inverse of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody>\n<tr height=\"35px\">\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"4\">\n${B}^{-1}=\\,\\,$\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">\n       {{field-1}}\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">\n$0$\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">\n$0$\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">\n$0$\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"35px\">\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n$0$\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n        {{field-2}}\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n        {{field-3}}\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n$0$\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"35px\">\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n$0$\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n        {{field-4}}\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n$0$\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n$0$\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"35px\">\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n$0$\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n$0$\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n$0$\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"] [:db/cas 17592186326903 :question/problem "The relationship between the advertising budget and sales (both in millions of dollars) of a company per year over several years is shown in the following table. \n\n| Advertising $(x)$ | **$2$** | **$2.5$** | **$3$** | **$3.5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Sales $(y)$ | $10$ | $12$ | $16$ | $20$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, what will be the total sales when the annual advertising budget is equal to $$5$ million, assuming the trend continues? \n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}31.5 & -11 \\\\ -11 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,{X}^{T}y=\\begin{bmatrix}58 \\\\ 168\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" "The relationship between the advertising budget and sales (both in millions of dollars) of a company per year over several years is shown in the following table. \n\n| Advertising $(x)$ | **$2$** | **$2.5$** | **$3$** | **$3.5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Sales $(y)$ | $10$ | $12$ | $16$ | $20$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, what will be the total sales when the annual advertising budget is equal to $\\$5$ million, assuming the trend continues? \n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}31.5 & -11 \\\\ -11 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,{X}^{T}y=\\begin{bmatrix}58 \\\\ 168\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*"] [:db/cas 17592186326903 :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2.5 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3.5\\end{bmatrix}}^{X}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 16 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix}}^{y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}31.5 & -11 \\\\ -11 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}58 \\\\ 168\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4.2 \\\\ 6.8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=-4.2+6.8x.$ \n\nFinally, to predict the value of the sales when the advertising budget is equal to $$5$ million, we substitute $x=5$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-4.2+6.8(5) \\\\  & =29.8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model, the total sales when the advertising budget equals $$5$ million will be $$29.8$ million. \n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=5$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable." "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2.5 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3.5\\end{bmatrix}}^{X}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 16 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix}}^{y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}31.5 & -11 \\\\ -11 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}58 \\\\ 168\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4.2 \\\\ 6.8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=-4.2+6.8x.$ \n\nFinally, to predict the value of the sales when the advertising budget is equal to $\\$5$ million, we substitute $x=5$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-4.2+6.8(5) \\\\  & =29.8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model, the total sales when the advertising budget equals $\\$5$ million will be $\\$29.8$ million. \n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=5$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable."] [:db/cas 17592186329667 :question/problem "In a particular city, about $30%$ of mornings are cloudy and $40%$ of days are rainy. \nIt is known that $60%$ of rainy days start with cloudy mornings. What is the probability that a randomly selected cloudy morning continues as a rainy day?" "In a particular city, about $30\\%$ of mornings are cloudy and $40\\%$ of days are rainy. \nIt is known that $60\\%$ of rainy days start with cloudy mornings. What is the probability that a randomly selected cloudy morning continues as a rainy day?"] [:db/cas 17592186329667 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be the event that a randomly selected morning is cloudy, and let $R$ be the event that a randomly selected day is rainy. Then, we require $P(R|C),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(R|C)=\\frac{P(C|R)P(R)}{P(C)}\n$$\n\n- $P(C)=0.3,$ since $30%$ of mornings are cloudy.\n\n- $P(R)=0.4,$ since $40%$ of days are rainy.\n\n- $P(C|R)=0.6,$ since $60%$ of rainy days start with cloudy mornings.\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R|C) & =\\frac{P(C|R)P(R)}{P(C)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.6\\cdot 0.4}{0.3} \\\\  & =0.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $C$ be the event that a randomly selected morning is cloudy, and let $R$ be the event that a randomly selected day is rainy. Then, we require $P(R|C),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(R|C)=\\frac{P(C|R)P(R)}{P(C)}\n$$\n\n- $P(C)=0.3,$ since $30\\%$ of mornings are cloudy.\n\n- $P(R)=0.4,$ since $40\\%$ of days are rainy.\n\n- $P(C|R)=0.6,$ since $60\\%$ of rainy days start with cloudy mornings.\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R|C) & =\\frac{P(C|R)P(R)}{P(C)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.6\\cdot 0.4}{0.3} \\\\  & =0.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329697 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=cot⁡x?$\n\n- The period is $\\pi .$ $$\n- It is increasing over $(0,\\pi ).$\n- $x=\\pi $ is a zero." "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=cot⁡x?$\n\n- The period is $\\pi .$ \n- It is increasing over $(0,\\pi ).$\n- $x=\\pi $ is a zero."] [:db/cas 17592186332798 :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given $y(1)$ and asked to find $y(3).$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=3$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-1}{2}=\\frac{2}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=2.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n \n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x+4y=2\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 2=10 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=10\\cdot 1=10 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+10=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this data to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $10$ | $10$ |\n| $2$ | $12$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x+4y=2\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 12=52 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=52\\cdot 1=52 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=12+52=64\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $10$ | $10$ |\n| $2$ | $12$ | $52$ | $52$ |\n| $3$ | $64$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)≈64.$" "First, note that we are given $y(1)$ and asked to find $y(3).$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=3$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-1}{2}=\\frac{2}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=2.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\n \n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x+4y=2\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 2=10 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=10\\cdot 1=10 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+10=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this data to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $10$ | $10$ |\n| $2$ | $12$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x+4y=2\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 12=52 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=52\\cdot 1=52 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=12+52=64\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $10$ | $10$ |\n| $2$ | $12$ | $52$ | $52$ |\n| $3$ | $64$ |  |  |\n\n \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)≈64.$"] [:db/cas 17592186332902 :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(2).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=2$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-0}{2}=\\frac{2}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=1.$ \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot 1=1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+1=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this data to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n \n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y=2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot 1=2 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+2=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $2$ | $4$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈4.$" "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(2).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=2$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-0}{2}=\\frac{2}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=1.$ \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot 1=1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+1=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this data to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\n \n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y=2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot 1=2 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+2=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $2$ | $4$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈4.$"] [:db/cas 17592186332903 :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(1).$ The distance from $x=3$ to $x=4$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be  \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-3}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(3)=2.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n \n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x+y=3+2=5 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=3+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+\\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $2$ | $5$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{7}{2}$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x+y=\\frac{7}{2}\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}=8 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=4 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{7}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=4 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=\\frac{9}{2}+4=\\frac{17}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $2$ | $5$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{7}{2}$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $8$ | $4$ |\n| $4$ | $\\frac{17}{2}$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that  $y(4)≈\\frac{17}{2}.$" "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(1).$ The distance from $x=3$ to $x=4$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be  \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-3}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(3)=2.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\n \n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x+y=3+2=5 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=3+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+\\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $2$ | $5$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{7}{2}$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x+y=\\frac{7}{2}\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}=8 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=4 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{7}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=4 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=\\frac{9}{2}+4=\\frac{17}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $2$ | $5$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{7}{2}$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $8$ | $4$ |\n| $4$ | $\\frac{17}{2}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that  $y(4)≈\\frac{17}{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186332969 :question/problem "![](images/c2/c241f6afc3978dfc8d1e2a048509a2e6fc7c91b0caf5ac4814a864edc3992d0c.png)\n\nChloe wants to paint a steel box that has the form of a rectangular prism. Two of the polygonal faces are $4\\,\\text{ft}\\times 4\\,\\text{ft}$ squares. If the cost to paint the surface is $$6.50$ per square foot, how much will it cost her in total? Note that Chloe does *not* need to paint the square sides." "![](images/c2/c241f6afc3978dfc8d1e2a048509a2e6fc7c91b0caf5ac4814a864edc3992d0c.png)\n\nChloe wants to paint a steel box that has the form of a rectangular prism. Two of the polygonal faces are $4\\,\\text{ft}\\times 4\\,\\text{ft}$ squares. If the cost to paint the surface is $\\$6.50$ per square foot, how much will it cost her in total? Note that Chloe does *not* need to paint the square sides."] [:db/cas 17592186332969 :question/worked-solution "The net of our prism is the following.\n\n![](images/7b/7b84a48933abb96f605476c6d703406227b0fe5301b1457a447cdbb3f58f0937.png)\n\n \n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nEach side face (apart from the square faces) of our solid is a rectangle, where the base is $7\\,\\text{ft}$ and the height is $4\\,\\text{ft}.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\text{Area of the rectangle}=7\\times 4=28\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, the total area of the side surfaces consists of $4$ rectangular faces. \n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Surface area of the sides} & =4\\times (\\text{Area of the rectangle}) \\\\  & =4\\times 28 \\\\  & =112\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the cost to paint the surface is $$6.50$ per square foot, the total amount that Chloe will have to pay is\n\n$$\n112\\times 6.50=$728.\n$$" "The net of our prism is the following.\n\n![](images/7b/7b84a48933abb96f605476c6d703406227b0fe5301b1457a447cdbb3f58f0937.png)\n\n \n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nEach side face (apart from the square faces) of our solid is a rectangle, where the base is $7\\,\\text{ft}$ and the height is $4\\,\\text{ft}.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\text{Area of the rectangle}=7\\times 4=28\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, the total area of the side surfaces consists of $4$ rectangular faces. \n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Surface area of the sides} & =4\\times (\\text{Area of the rectangle}) \\\\  & =4\\times 28 \\\\  & =112\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the cost to paint the surface is $\\$6.50$ per square foot, the total amount that Chloe will have to pay is\n\n$$\n112\\times 6.50=$728.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332971 :question/problem "In a particular business bank account, the rate at which money is being deposited into the account is given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=600(2t+1)^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in dollars per month, and $t>0$ is the number of months since the account was opened. When the account was first opened, the business owners placed an initial deposit of $$1\\,000$ into the account.\n\nHow much money will be in the account after $3$ months?" "In a particular business bank account, the rate at which money is being deposited into the account is given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=600(2t+1)^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in dollars per month, and $t>0$ is the number of months since the account was opened. When the account was first opened, the business owners placed an initial deposit of $\\$1\\,000$ into the account.\n\nHow much money will be in the account after $3$ months?"] [:db/cas 17592186332971 :question/worked-solution "We're given a **rate of change** in the amount of money $r(t).$ \n\nTo find the **total amount** of money in the account after $t$ months (call it $T(t)$), we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =T(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}r(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1\\,000+{\\int }_{0}^{t}600(2x+1)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo be sure that $T$ is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration. \n\nWe now let $t=3,$ and evaluate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(3) & =1\\,000+{\\int }_{0}^{3}600(2x+1)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1\\,000+600{\\int }_{0}^{3}(2x+1)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1\\,000+\\frac{600}{2\\cdot 4}{(2t+1)^{4}|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =1\\,000+75{(2t+1)^{4}|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =1\\,000+75[(2(3)+1)^{4}-(2(0)+1)^{4}] \\\\  & =1\\,000+75[{7}^{4}-{1}^{4}] \\\\  & =1\\,000+75[2\\,401-1] \\\\  & =1\\,000+75[2\\,400] \\\\  & =181\\,000\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there will be $$181\\,000$ in the account after $3$ months." "We're given a **rate of change** in the amount of money $r(t).$ \n\nTo find the **total amount** of money in the account after $t$ months (call it $T(t)$), we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =T(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}r(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1\\,000+{\\int }_{0}^{t}600(2x+1)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo be sure that $T$ is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration. \n\nWe now let $t=3,$ and evaluate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(3) & =1\\,000+{\\int }_{0}^{3}600(2x+1)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1\\,000+600{\\int }_{0}^{3}(2x+1)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1\\,000+\\frac{600}{2\\cdot 4}{(2t+1)^{4}|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =1\\,000+75{(2t+1)^{4}|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =1\\,000+75[(2(3)+1)^{4}-(2(0)+1)^{4}] \\\\  & =1\\,000+75[{7}^{4}-{1}^{4}] \\\\  & =1\\,000+75[2\\,401-1] \\\\  & =1\\,000+75[2\\,400] \\\\  & =181\\,000\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there will be $\\$181\\,000$ in the account after $3$ months."] [:db/cas 17592186335991 :question/problem "Calculate $det(H)$, if\n    $$\n\\displaystyle H=\\begin{bmatrix}-2r & 3s \\\\ -2t & 3u\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" "Calculate $det(H)$, if\n$$\n\\displaystyle H=\\begin{bmatrix}-2r & 3s \\\\ -2t & 3u\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186354331 :question/worked-solution "A binomial distribution models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (where each outcome is either success or failure).\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable “The number of members of a school band” could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of school band members cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\n- Random variable “The number of goals scored by a soccer player in $10$ penalties if the probability of scoring any penalty is $65%$” could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of goals in $10$ penalties can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $10$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable “The number of victories of a chess player in $5$ matches if the probability of the player winning any individual game is $45%$” could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of wins in $5$ matches can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $5$ independent experiments.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The number of goals scored by a soccer player in $10$ penalties if the probability of scoring any penalty is $65%$”; “The number of victories of a chess player in $5$ matches if the probability of the player winning any individual game is $45%$” only." "A binomial distribution models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (where each outcome is either success or failure).\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable “The number of members of a school band” could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of school band members cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\n- Random variable “The number of goals scored by a soccer player in $10$ penalties if the probability of scoring any penalty is $65\\%$” could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of goals in $10$ penalties can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $10$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable “The number of victories of a chess player in $5$ matches if the probability of the player winning any individual game is $45\\%$” could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of wins in $5$ matches can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $5$ independent experiments.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The number of goals scored by a soccer player in $10$ penalties if the probability of scoring any penalty is $65\\%$”; “The number of victories of a chess player in $5$ matches if the probability of the player winning any individual game is $45\\%$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186354342 :question/problem "A painter at an exhibition has a $35%$ chance of selling each of his works of art. What is the probability that when exhibiting $14$ works of art, the painter sells $2$ or more? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." "A painter at an exhibition has a $35\\%$ chance of selling each of his works of art. What is the probability that when exhibiting $14$ works of art, the painter sells $2$ or more? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186354342 :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of works of art sold.\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=14$ works of art\n\n- the probability of selling each work is $p=35%=0.35$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(14,0.35).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{14}{x})(0.35)^{x}(1-0.35)^{14-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{14}{x})(0.35)^{x}(0.65)^{14-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that the painter sells $2$ or more works of art. This corresponds to $P(X\\ge 2).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 2)=P(X=2)+P(X=3)+…+P(X=14)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =1-P(X<2) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{14}{0})(0.35)^{0}(0.65)^{14-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{14}{1})(0.35)^{1}(0.65)^{14-1}] \\\\  & =1-[(0.65)^{14}+14(0.35)(0.65)^{13}] \\\\  & =1-0.020\\,519 \\\\  & =0.979\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of works of art sold.\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=14$ works of art\n\n- the probability of selling each work is $p=35\\%=0.35$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(14,0.35).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{14}{x})(0.35)^{x}(1-0.35)^{14-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{14}{x})(0.35)^{x}(0.65)^{14-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that the painter sells $2$ or more works of art. This corresponds to $P(X\\ge 2).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 2)=P(X=2)+P(X=3)+…+P(X=14)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =1-P(X<2) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{14}{0})(0.35)^{0}(0.65)^{14-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{14}{1})(0.35)^{1}(0.65)^{14-1}] \\\\  & =1-[(0.65)^{14}+14(0.35)(0.65)^{13}] \\\\  & =1-0.020\\,519 \\\\  & =0.979\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186354535 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{4},\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the second row of the table below if the step size is $Δx=2?$ \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| $$ | $$ | $$ | $\\,?\\,$ |" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{4},\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the second row of the table below if the step size is $Δx=2?$ \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n|  |  |  | $\\,?\\,$ |"] [:db/cas 17592186354535 :question/worked-solution "To get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =1+2 \\\\  & =3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =2+2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| $3$ | $4$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row), and we compute \n$Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot {4}^{2}}{4} \\\\  & =12 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =12\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| $3$ | $4$ | $12$ | $24$ |" "To get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =1+2 \\\\  & =3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =2+2 \\\\  & =4