|\n| $-3$ | $1$ | $8$ | $$ |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =8\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-3$ | $1$ | $8$ | $24$ |" "First, because the initial condition is $y(-3)=1,$ we fill $y=1$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-3$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-x+5y \\\\  & =-(-3)+5\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-3$ | $1$ | $8$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =8\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-3$ | $1$ | $8$ | $24$ |"] [:db/cas 17592186357368 :question/problem "A student answers a multiple-choice test. Each question of the test has four possible answers, and only one choice is correct. The student knows the correct answer for $60%$ of the questions. If the student decides to select an answer at random for the questions that she does not know the answer to, what is the probability that a randomly selected question on the test is correct?" "A student answers a multiple-choice test. Each question of the test has four possible answers, and only one choice is correct. The student knows the correct answer for $60\\%$ of the questions. If the student decides to select an answer at random for the questions that she does not know the answer to, what is the probability that a randomly selected question on the test is correct?"] [:db/cas 17592186357368 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be the event that the selected question is correct, and let $K$ be the event that the student knew the answer. Then ${K}^{′}$ is the event that the student did not know the answer.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected question is correct, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(C)=P(C|K)P(K)+P(C|{K}^{′})P({K}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since the student knows the answer to $60%$ of the questions, we have\n\n$$\nP(K)=0.6,\\,P({K}^{′})=0.4.\n$$\n\n- Since each question has four possible answers, we have\n\n$$\nP(C|K)=1,\\,P(C|{K}^{′})=\\frac{1}{4}=0.25.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected question on the test is correct is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(C) & =P(C|K)P(K)+P(C|{K}^{′})P({K}^{′}) \\\\  & =(1)(0.6)+(0.25)(0.4) \\\\  & =0.6+0.1 \\\\  & =0.70.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $C$ be the event that the selected question is correct, and let $K$ be the event that the student knew the answer. Then ${K}^{′}$ is the event that the student did not know the answer.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected question is correct, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(C)=P(C|K)P(K)+P(C|{K}^{′})P({K}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since the student knows the answer to $60\\%$ of the questions, we have\n\n$$\nP(K)=0.6,\\,P({K}^{′})=0.4.\n$$\n\n- Since each question has four possible answers, we have\n\n$$\nP(C|K)=1,\\,P(C|{K}^{′})=\\frac{1}{4}=0.25.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected question on the test is correct is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(C) & =P(C|K)P(K)+P(C|{K}^{′})P({K}^{′}) \\\\  & =(1)(0.6)+(0.25)(0.4) \\\\  & =0.6+0.1 \\\\  & =0.70.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357369 :question/problem "A company manufactures electronic components. Of the components manufactured, $45%$ are produced by machine $A$, and the remaining $55%$ are produced by machine $B.$ If $8%$ of the components produced by machine $A$ are defective and $4%$ of the components produced by machine $B$ are defective, what is the probability that a randomly selected component is defective?" "A company manufactures electronic components. Of the components manufactured, $45\\%$ are produced by machine $A$, and the remaining $55\\%$ are produced by machine $B.$ If $8\\%$ of the components produced by machine $A$ are defective and $4\\%$ of the components produced by machine $B$ are defective, what is the probability that a randomly selected component is defective?"] [:db/cas 17592186357369 :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the event that the selected component is defective, and let $A$ be the event that the component was produced by machine $A.$ Then, ${A}^{′}$ is the event that the component was produced by machine $B.$\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected component is defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(D)=P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since machine $A$ produces $45%$ of the components and $B$ the remaining $55%$, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.45,\\,P({A}^{′})=0.55.\n$$\n\n- Since $8%$ of the components produced by machine $A$ are defective, and $4%$ of the components produced by machine $B$ are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.08,\\,P(D|{A}^{′})=0.04.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected component is defective is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′}) \\\\  & =(0.08)(0.45)+(0.04)(0.55) \\\\  & =0.058.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $D$ be the event that the selected component is defective, and let $A$ be the event that the component was produced by machine $A.$ Then, ${A}^{′}$ is the event that the component was produced by machine $B.$\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected component is defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(D)=P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since machine $A$ produces $45\\%$ of the components and $B$ the remaining $55\\%$, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.45,\\,P({A}^{′})=0.55.\n$$\n\n- Since $8\\%$ of the components produced by machine $A$ are defective, and $4\\%$ of the components produced by machine $B$ are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.08,\\,P(D|{A}^{′})=0.04.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected component is defective is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′}) \\\\  & =(0.08)(0.45)+(0.04)(0.55) \\\\  & =0.058.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357373 :question/problem "In a gas station, $42%$ of the clients buy regular gasoline while $58%$ buy premium gasoline. If $62%$ of the clients that buy regular gasoline paid with a credit card while $55%$ of the clients that buy premium gasoline paid with a credit card, what is the probability that a randomly selected client paid with a credit card? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." "In a gas station, $42\\%$ of the clients buy regular gasoline while $58\\%$ buy premium gasoline. If $62\\%$ of the clients that buy regular gasoline paid with a credit card while $55\\%$ of the clients that buy premium gasoline paid with a credit card, what is the probability that a randomly selected client paid with a credit card? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186357373 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be the event that the chosen person paid with a credit card, and let $R$ be the event that the person bought regular gasoline. Then ${R}^{′}$ is the event that the person bought premium gasoline.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected person paid with a credit card, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(C)=P(C|R)P(R)+P(C|{R}^{′})P({R}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $42%$ of the clients buy regular gasoline while $58%$ buy premium gasoline, we have\n\n$$\nP(R)=0.42,\\,P({R}^{′})=0.58.\n$$\n\n- Since $62%$ of the clients that buy regular gasoline paid with a credit card while $55%$ of the clients that buy premium gasoline paid with a credit card, we have\n\n$$\nP(C|R)=0.62,\\,P(C|{R}^{′})=0.55.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected client paid with a credit card is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(C) & =P(C|R)P(R)+P(C|{R}^{′})P({R}^{′}) \\\\  & =(0.62)(0.42)+(0.55)(0.58) \\\\  & ≈0.579\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." "Let $C$ be the event that the chosen person paid with a credit card, and let $R$ be the event that the person bought regular gasoline. Then ${R}^{′}$ is the event that the person bought premium gasoline.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected person paid with a credit card, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(C)=P(C|R)P(R)+P(C|{R}^{′})P({R}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $42\\%$ of the clients buy regular gasoline while $58\\%$ buy premium gasoline, we have\n\n$$\nP(R)=0.42,\\,P({R}^{′})=0.58.\n$$\n\n- Since $62\\%$ of the clients that buy regular gasoline paid with a credit card while $55\\%$ of the clients that buy premium gasoline paid with a credit card, we have\n\n$$\nP(C|R)=0.62,\\,P(C|{R}^{′})=0.55.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected client paid with a credit card is\n\n$$\n\