 the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(S|{A}^{′})$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(S) & =P(S|A)P(A)+P(S|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′}) \\\\ 0.36 & =(0.30)(0.40)+P(S|{A}^{′})(0.60) \\\\ 0.36 & =0.12+0.6P(S|{A}^{′}) \\\\ 0.6P(S|{A}^{′}) & =0.24 \\\\ P(S|{A}^{′}) & =0.40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected email from account $B$ is spam is $0.40.$" "Let $S$ be the event that the chosen email is spam, and let $A$ be the event that the email selected is from account $A.$ Then ${A}^{′}$ is the event that the email selected is from account $B.$\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected email is spam, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(S)=P(S|A)P(A)+P(S|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $40\\%$ of the emails are received in account $A$ and the remaining $60\\%$ in account $B,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.40,\\,P({A}^{′})=0.60.\n$$\n\n- Since $30\\%$ of the emails received in account $A$ are spam, we have \n\n$$\nP(S|A)=0.30.\n$$\n\n- Since $36\\%$ of the emails received in both accounts are spam, we have \n\n$$\nP(S)=0.36.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(S|{A}^{′})$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(S) & =P(S|A)P(A)+P(S|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′}) \\\\ 0.36 & =(0.30)(0.40)+P(S|{A}^{′})(0.60) \\\\ 0.36 & =0.12+0.6P(S|{A}^{′}) \\\\ 0.6P(S|{A}^{′}) & =0.24 \\\\ P(S|{A}^{′}) & =0.40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected email from account $B$ is spam is $0.40.$"] [:db/cas 17592186357381 :question/problem "In a gas station, $55%$ of the customers buy regular gasoline while $45%$ buy premium gasoline. If $60%$ of the customers that buy regular gasoline usually pay with a credit card and the probability that a randomly selected customer pays with a credit card is $0.645,$ what is the probability that a randomly selected customer who buys premium gasoline usually pays with a credit card? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." "In a gas station, $55\\%$ of the customers buy regular gasoline while $45\\%$ buy premium gasoline. If $60\\%$ of the customers that buy regular gasoline usually pay with a credit card and the probability that a randomly selected customer pays with a credit card is $0.645,$ what is the probability that a randomly selected customer who buys premium gasoline usually pays with a credit card? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186357381 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be the event that the chosen person pays with a credit card, and let $R$ be the event that the person buys regular gas. Then ${R}^{′}$ is the event that the person buys premium gas.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected person pays with a credit card, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(C)=P(C|R)P(R)+P(C|{R}^{′})P({R}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $55%$ of the customers buy regular gasoline while $45%$ buy premium gasoline, we have\n\n$$\nP(R)=0.55,\\,P({R}^{′})=0.45.\n$$\n\n- Since $60%$ of the customers that buy regular gasoline pay with a credit card, we have\n\n$$\nP(C|R)=0.60.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected customer pays with a credit card is $0.645,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(C)=0.645.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(C|{R}^{′}),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(C) & =P(C|R)P(R)+P(C|{R}^{′})P({R}^{′}) \\\\ 0.645 & =(0.60)(0.55)+P(C|{R}^{′})(0.45) \\\\ 0.645 & =0.33+0.45P(C|{R}^{′}) \\\\ 0.45P(C|{R}^{′}) & =0.315 \\\\ P(C|{R}^{′}) & =0.70.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected customer who buys premium gasoline usually pays with a credit card is $0.70.$" "Let $C$ be the event that the chosen person pays with 