ow if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $e$ | $8$ | $4$ |\n|  |  |  | $\\,?\\,$ |"] [:db/cas 17592186357382 :question/worked-solution "To get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =2+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =e+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $e$ | $8$ | $4$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $e+4$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row), and we compute \n$Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\  & =4\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+4) \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+4) \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+4)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $e$ | $8$ | $4$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $e+4$ | $10\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+4)$ | $5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+4)$ |" "To get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =2+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =e+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $e$ | $8$ | $4$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $e+4$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row), and we compute \n$Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\  & =4\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+4) \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+4) \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+4)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $e$ | $8$ | $4$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $e+4$ | $10\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+4)$ | $5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+4)$ |"] [:db/cas 17592186357392 :question/worked-solution "A binomial distribution models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (where each outcome is either success or failure).\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable “The number of baskets made by a basketball player attempting three free throws if the probability of scoring any free throw is $65%$” could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of baskets in $3$ throws can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $3$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable “The number of defective items in a random sample of $100$ items produced by a machine” could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of defective items in a random sample of $100$ items can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $100$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable “The number of views of a video on a popular video channel” could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of views cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The number of baskets made by a basketball player attempting three free throws if the probability of scoring any free throw is $65%$”; “The number of defective items in a random sample of $100$ items produced by a machine” only." "A binomial distribution models the number of success