/worked-solution "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overset{―}{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma ,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma \n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\overset{―}{x}=64.3,$ $z=-1,$ and $\\sigma =5.25$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\  & =64.3+(-1)\\cdot 5.25 \\\\  & =64.3-5.25 \\\\  & =59.05.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the minimum passing score is $59.05%.$" "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overset{―}{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma ,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma \n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\overset{―}{x}=64.3,$ $z=-1,$ and $\\sigma =5.25$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\  & =64.3+(-1)\\cdot 5.25 \\\\  & =64.3-5.25 \\\\  & =59.05.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the minimum passing score is $59.05\\%.$"] [:db/cas 17592186362465 :question/problem "Write the space $R$ bounded by the cylinder as a type I region in three-dimensional space.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $R={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,\\le z\\le \\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$},$ where\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-3}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,1}.$" "Write the space $R$ bounded by the cylinder as a type I region in three-dimensional space.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$R={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,\\le z\\le \\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$},$ where\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$D={(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-3}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,1}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186362472 :question/problem "The equation of the heater's surface in polar coordinates is given by $r=f(\\theta ,z),$ where $(\\theta ,z)\\in C.$ Find $f(\\theta ,z)$ and $C.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $f(\\theta ,z)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$,\\,C=[$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-3}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$)\\times [$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-4}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-5}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$]$" "The equation of the heater's surface in polar coordinates is given by $r=f(\\theta ,z),$ where $(\\theta ,z)\\in C.$ Find $f(\\theta ,z)$ and $C.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$f(\\theta ,z)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$,\\,C=[$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-3}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$)\\times [$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-4}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-5}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$]$"] [:db/cas 17592186362483 :question/problem "The cost, in dollars, to manufacture the mesh for a cylindrical heater $r=f(\\theta ,z),$ where $(\\theta ,z)\\in C,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2\\pi }{∬}_{C}(\\frac{r}{3}+\\frac{\\theta }{\\pi }+2z)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nHow much did it cost to make the mesh for the cylindrical heater?\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The total cost is $\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n." "The cost, in dollars, to manufacture the mesh for a cylindrical heater $r=f(\\theta ,z),$ where $(\\theta ,z)\\in C,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2\\pi }{∬}_{C}(\\frac{r}{3}+\\frac{\\theta }{\\pi }+2z)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nHow much did it cost to make the mesh for the cylindrical heater?\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The total cost is \\$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n."] [:db/cas 17592186362483 :question/worked-solution "We found that the surface of the heater can be described in cylindrical polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nr=3,\\,(\\theta ,z)\\in C,\n$$\n\nwhere\n$C=[0,2\\pi )\\times [0,3].$\n\nHence, the cost to manufacture its mesh is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2\\pi }{∬}_{C}(1+\\frac{\\theta }{\\pi }+2z)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nUsing the linearity property of double integrals, we can write\n\n$$\n{∬}_{C}(1+\\frac{\\theta }{\\pi }+2z)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{C}\\text{d}A+{∬}_{C}\\frac{\\theta }{\\pi }\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{C}2z\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe function under the first integral is constant, so we have\n\n$$\n{∬}_{C}\\text{d}A=\\text{Area}(C)=2\\pi \\cdot 3=6\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe function under the second integral is independent of $z.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{C}\\frac{\\theta }{\\pi }\\,\\text{d}A & =\\frac{(3-0)}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\pi }\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{\\theta }^{2}]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2\\pi }\\cdot {[{\\theta }^{2}]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2\\pi }((2\\pi )^{2}-0) \\\\  & =6\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function under the third integral is independent of $\\theta .$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{C}2z\\,\\text{d}A & =(2\\pi -0){\\int }_{0}^{3}2z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot {[{z}^{2}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =2\\pi ({3}^{2}-0) \\\\  & =18\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, combining our results, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2\\pi }{∬}_{C}(1+\\frac{\\theta }{\\pi }+2z)\\,\\text{d}A=\\frac{1}{2\\pi }(6\\pi +6\\pi +18\\pi )=15.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cost to manufacture the mesh of the cylindrical heater is $$15.$" "We found that the surface of the heater can be described in cylindrical polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nr=3,\\,(\\theta ,z)\\in C,\n$$\n\nwhere\n$C=[0,2\\pi )\\times [0,3].$\n\nHence, the cost to manufacture its mesh is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2\\pi }{∬}_{C}(1+\\frac{\\theta }{\\pi }+2z)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nUsing the linearity property of double integrals, we can write\n\n$$\n{∬}_{C}(1+\\frac{\\theta }{\\pi }+2z)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{C}\\text{d}A+{∬}_{C}\\frac{\\theta }{\\pi }\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{C}2z\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe function under the first integral is constant, so we have\n\n$$\n{∬}_{C}\\text{d}A=\\text{Area}(C)=2\\pi \\cdot 3=6\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe function under the second integral is independent of $z.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{C}\\frac{\\theta }{\\pi }\\,\\text{d}A & =\\frac{(3-0)}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\pi }\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{\\theta }^{2}]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2\\pi }\\cdot {[{\\theta }^{2}]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2\\pi }((2\\pi )^{2}-0) \\\\  & =6\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function under the third integral is independent of $\\theta .$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{C}2z\\,\\text{d}A & =(2\\pi -0){\\int }_{0}^{3}2z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot {[{z}^{2}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =2\\pi ({3}^{2}-0) \\\\  & =18\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, combining our results, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2\\pi }{∬}_{C}(1+\\frac{\\theta }{\\pi }+2z)\\,\\text{d}A=\\frac{1}{2\\pi }(6\\pi +6\\pi +18\\pi )=15.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cost to manufacture the mesh of the cylindrical heater is $\\$15.$"] [:db/cas 17592186362489 :question/problem "A heating element $E$ lies on the intersection of the cylindrical heater and the plane $z=2.$ Use your answer to part 4 to find an outward-pointing unit normal vector $n(x,y)$ to $E$ that lies within this plane. Express your answer in terms of $x$ and $y.$\n\n*Hint: The matrix ${R}_{z}(ϕ)$ representing a counterclockwise rotation of $ϕ$ radians about the $z$-axis is given by*\n\n$$\n{R}_{z}(ϕ)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos