oint.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Consider the curve ${x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+y=3.$ Given that $x=x(t),\\text{ }y=y(t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=7$ on the curve at the point $(1,3),$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186369539 :question/problem "Consider the following game featuring two fair six-sided dice rolled simultaneously. If the sum of the numbers on the dice is $6,$ the player wins $$6.$ Otherwise, the player loses $$1.$ What is the expected profit from each roll in this game? Round your answer to three decimal places." "Consider the following game featuring two fair six-sided dice rolled simultaneously. If the sum of the numbers on the dice is $6,$ the player wins $\\$6.$ Otherwise, the player loses $\\$1.$ What is the expected profit from each roll in this game? Round your answer to three decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186369539 :question/worked-solution "Let $p$ represent the profit on a single roll of the game. On each roll in the game, there are two possible outcomes:\n\n- If the sum of the outcomes is $6$ ($(1,5),(2,4),(3,3),(4,2)$, or $(5,1)$), the player wins $$6,$ so we have $p=6.$\n\n- If the sum of the outcomes is not $6,$ the player loses $$1,$ so we have $p=-1.$\n\nThe probabilities for the outcomes are\n\n$$$\n\\displaystyle \nP(6)=\\frac{5}{36},\\,P(-1)=\\frac{31}{36}.\n\n$$$\n\nLet's summarize this in a table. If $p$ is the profit (in dollars) on a single game, and $f(p)$ is the probability mass function for $p,$ then we have the following:\n\n| $f(p)$ | $\\frac{5}{36}$ | $\\frac{31}{36}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $p$ | $6$ | $-1$ |\n\nTherefore, the expected value of $p$ is given by\n\n$$$\n\\displaystyle \n\\begin{aligned}E[p] & =6\\cdot f(6)+(-1)\\cdot f(-1) \\\\  & =6\\left(\\frac{5}{36}\\right)+(-1)\\left(\\frac{31}{36}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{30}{36}-\\frac{31}{36} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{36} \\\\  & ≈-0.028\\end{aligned}\n\n$$$\n\nTherefore, the expected profit is $-$0.028.$" "Let $p$ represent the profit on a single roll of the game. On each roll in the game, there are two possible outcomes:\n\n- If the sum of the outcomes is $6$ ($(1,5),(2,4),(3,3),(4,2)$, or $(5,1)$), the player wins $\\$6,$ so we have $p=6.$\n\n- If the sum of the outcomes is not $6,$ the player loses $\\$1,$ so we have $p=-1.$\n\nThe probabilities for the outcomes are\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \nP(6)=\\frac{5}{36},\\,P(-1)=\\frac{31}{36}.\n\n$$\n\nLet's summarize this in a table. If $p$ is the profit (in dollars) on a single game, and $f(p)$ is the probability mass function for $p,$ then we have the following:\n\n| $f(p)$ | $\\frac{5}{36}$ | $\\frac{31}{36}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $p$ | $6$ | $-1$ |\n\nTherefore, the expected value of $p$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \n\\begin{aligned}E[p] & =6\\cdot f(6)+(-1)\\cdot f(-1) \\\\  & =6\\left(\\frac{5}{36}\\right)+(-1)\\left(\\frac{31}{36}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{30}{36}-\\frac{31}{36} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{36} \\\\  & ≈-0.028\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected profit is $-\\$0.028.$"] [:db/cas 17592186370579 :question/problem "At a carnival, a ring toss game costs $$2$ per play. If the player successfully lands a ring, they win $$30.$ Otherwise, the player wins nothing. Suppose the probability of winning on any single play is $\\frac{1}{20}.$ What is the expected profit, rounded to the nearest dollar, for a player who plays forty games in a row?\n\nThe expected profit in dollars is $E(p)=$\n{{field-1}}" "At a carnival, a ring toss game costs $\\$2$ per play. If the player successfully lands a ring, they win $\\$30.$ Otherwise, the player wins nothing. Suppose the probability of winning on any single play is $\\frac{1}{20}.$ What is the expected profit, rounded to the nearest dollar, for a player who plays forty games in a row?\n\nThe expected profit in dollars is $E(p)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186370654 :question/problem