d>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"] [:db/cas 17592186410435 :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{5}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼Po(\\theta )$ and unknown rate parameter $\\theta .$ For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{5},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{x}_{i}=6,\\,{\\prod }_{i=1}^{5}({x}_{i})!=2.\n$$\n\nFind the likelihood function of the sample.\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a Poisson distribution is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\theta }^{x}{e}^{-\\theta }}{x!},\\,x=0,1,2,…\n$$\n\n$$" "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{5}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼Po(\\theta )$ and unknown rate parameter $\\theta .$ For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{5},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{x}_{i}=6,\\,{\\prod }_{i=1}^{5}({x}_{i})!=2.\n$$\n\nFind the likelihood function of the sample.\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a Poisson distribution is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\theta }^{x}{e}^{-\\theta }}{x!},\\,x=0,1,2,…\n$$\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186411560 :question/problem "It is known that $41%$ of a given population have blood type O+. What is the probability that at most $3$ of $10$ randomly selected people have blood type O+? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." "It is known that $41\\%$ of a given population have blood type O+. What is the probability that at most $3$ of $10$ randomly selected people have blood type O+? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186411562 :question/worked-solution "A binomial distribution models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (where each outcome is either success or failure).\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable “The ages of $20$ soccer players” could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The ages cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\n- Random variable “The price of a pair of pants in a department store” could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The prices cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\n- Random variable “The number of wins by a basketball team in a $30$-game season if the probability of the team winning any individual game is $55%$” could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of wins in $30$ games can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $30$ independent experiments.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The number of wins by a basketball team in a $30$-game season if the probability of the team winning any individual game is $55%$” only." "A binomial distribution models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (where each outcome is either success or failure).\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable “The ages of $20$ soccer players” could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The ages cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\n- Random variable “The price of a pair of pants in a department store” could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The prices cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\n- Random variable “The number of wins by a basketball team in a $30$-game season if the probability of the team winning any individual game is $55\\%$” could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of wins in $30$ games can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $30$ independent experiments.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The number of wins by a basketball team in a $30$-game season if the probability of the team winning any individual game is $55\\%$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186411564 :question/problem "$70%$ of union members are in favor of a specific change to their conditions of employment. A random sample of $6$ members is taken. What is the probability that at most $2$ members are in favor of the changed conditions? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." "$70\\%$ of union members are in favor of a specific change to their conditions of employment. A random sample of $6$ members is taken. What is the probability that at most $2$ members are in favor of the changed conditions? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186411564 :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be the number of members in the sample in favor of the change.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=6$ members\n\n- since $70%$ of union members are in favor of the change, the probability of selecting a member in favor of the change is $p=70%=0.7$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(6,0.7).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x}){(0.7)}^{x}{(1-0.7)}^{6-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x}){(0.7)}^{x}{(0.3)}^{6-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that at most two members are in favor of the changed conditions. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2) & =P(X=0)+P(X=1)+P(X=2) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{0}){(0.7)}^{0}{(0.3)}^{6-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{1}){(0.7)}^{1}{(0.3)}^{6-1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{2}){(0.7)}^{2}{(0.3)}^{6-2} \\\\  & =1{(0.7)}^{0}{(0.3)}^{6}+6{(0.7)}^{1}{(0.3)}^{5}+15{(0.7)}^{2}{(0.3)}^{4} \\\\  & ≈0.070\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "Let the random variable $X$ be the number of members in the sample in favor of the change.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=6$ members\n\n- since $70\\%$ of union members are in favor of the change, the probability of selecting a member in favor of the change is $p=70\\%=0.7$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(6,0.7).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x}){(0.7)}^{x}{(1-0.7)}^{6-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x}){(0.7)}^{x}{(0.3)}^{6-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that at most two members are in favor of the changed conditions. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2) & =P(X=0)+P(X=1)+P(X=2) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{0}){(0.7)}^{0}{(0.3)}^{6-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{1}){(0.7)}^{1}{(0.3)}^{6-1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{2}){(0.7)}^{2}{(0.3)}^{6-2} \\\\  & =1{(0.7)}^{0}{(0.3)}^{6}+6{(0.7)}^{1}{(0.3)}^{5}+15{(0.7)}^{2}{(0.3)}^{4} \\\\  & ≈0.070\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186411566 :question/problem "In a bag containing candies, there is a $70%$ chance of getting a red candy when a random candy is taken. If $16$ candies are removed from the bag, what is the probability that fewer than $15$ candies are red? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places.\n\nYou may assume that the bag contains a large number of candies and that the probability of selecting a red candy is the same on each trial." "In a bag containing candies, there is a $70\\%$ chance of getting a red candy when a random candy is taken. If $16$ candies are removed from the bag, what is the probability that fewer than $15$ candies are red? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places.\n\nYou may assume that the bag contains